hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"7261","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: One who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan may eat and drink if he wishes."
"7262","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: If he has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan then eats and drinks, there is a single expiation like the expiation for intercourse."
"7263","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, concerning one who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Free a slave. He said: I cannot find. He said: Then give something in charity. He said: I know only that he said: Then make up a day in its place."
"7264","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the two circumcised parts meet, the fast is nullified."
"7265","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning one who eats in Ramadan deliberately, he said: Like the one who has intercourse."
"7266","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a man who ate in Ramadan deliberately. He said: Upon him is fasting a month. He said: I said: Two days? He said: Fasting a month. He said: So I counted days, and he said: Fasting a month."
"7267","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: He makes up a day and seeks Allah's forgiveness."
"7268","From Ibn Uyaynah, from an elder of Bajilah, he said: I asked al-Shabi about a man who broke fast one day in Ramadan. He said: What do the strict ones say about it? He said: Then al-Shabi said: He fasts a day in its place and seeks Allah's forgiveness. And Abu Hanifah said it, from Hammad, from Ibrahim."
"7269","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Yala ibn Hakim, from Said ibn Jubayr, and from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, they both said: We know nothing except that he makes up a day. From al-Thawri, from one who heard Ibrahim saying something like that."
"7270","And Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman said: He fasts twelve days."
"7271","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: That a man broke fast one day of Ramadan, then fasted three thousand days."
"7272","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn al-Mutawwas, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever breaks fast one day of Ramadan without a concession from Allah, fasting the whole lifetime will not make it up, even if he fasts it."
"7273","From al-Thawri, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from Mughirah ibn Abd Allah al-Yashkuri, from a man, he said: Ibn Masud said: Whoever breaks fast one day of Ramadan without a concession from Allah will meet Allah with it, even if he fasts the whole lifetime; if He wills He forgives him, and if He wills He punishes him."
"7274","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He disliked that a person insert anything in Ramadan during the day; if he does, let him replace a day, but let him not break that day."
"7275","From al-Thawri, he said: One who is given an enema with wine breaks fast, but the hadd punishment is not applied."
"7276","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Asim, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) using the siwak while fasting, more times than I can count."
"7277","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a person has intercourse with his wife while making up Ramadan, he replaces that day, and there is no expiation upon him. I said: And if he fondles her? He said: He also replaces that day, but he should not break fast."
"7278","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata permitting a person suffering thirst while making up Ramadan to break fast. Ibn Jurayj said: And I ordered a person, and he asked him: Does the makeup of Ramadan hold the same place as voluntary fasting? He said: Yes."
"7279","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they both said: When a person is invited to food while fasting, let him say: I am fasting."
"7280","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Ibn Masud, he said: When food or drink is offered to one of you while he is fasting, let him say: I am fasting."
"7281","Al-Thawri reported, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Asim, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) using the siwak while fasting, more times than I can count."
"7282","From al-Thawri, from Abu Nahik, from Ziyad ibn Hudayr al-Asadi, he said: I never saw a man more constant in siwak than Umar ibn al-Khattab while fasting, but with a stick that had withered, meaning dry."
"7283","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: That Abu Hurayrah said: I have made my mouth bleed today while fasting with the siwak, twice."
"7284","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May the fasting person use the siwak? He said: Yes. It was said to him: Does he swallow his saliva? He said: I said: If he does so, does he break fast? He said: No, but he is forbidden from that. He said: I said: If he swallows it while it is said to him that he is forbidden from that? He said: He has broken fast then - he said that more than once."
"7285","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi: That Ibn Umar used to use the siwak while fasting, when he went out to the noon prayer."
"7286","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Maymun ibn Mihran disliking the siwak for the fasting person at the end of the day; so I asked al-Hasan, and he said: There is no harm in it at the end of the day; it is only purification, so let him use siwak at its beginning and its end."
"7287","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is forbidden regarding the siwak? He said: If the siwak is dry, no water comes from it. I said: What is this called the water of the siwak? He said: The saliva that is on it, coming from the head and the mouth. I said: If the siwak is dry with no juice? He said: Yes."
"7288","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Hisham informed me, from Urwah: That he used to brush with a moist siwak while fasting."
"7289","From al-Thawri and others, from Layth, from Mujahid: That he saw no harm in the moist siwak for the fasting person, and this is what al-Thawri holds."
"7290","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, he said: Do not brush with a moist siwak while fasting, for some of its taste enters your throat."
"7291","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: He used to dislike the moist palm stalk with which the fasting person brushes, because of its taste."
"7292","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Mujahid: That he used to dislike the siwak for the fasting person at the end of the day."
"7293","From al-Thawri, from Ubaydah, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in the siwak at the beginning of the day and its end for the fasting person."
"7294","From one of his companions, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no harm in the green siwak for the fasting person. He said: I know only that Maslamah informed me of it."
"7295","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May the fasting person chew gum? He said: No. I said: He spits out the gum's saliva and does not swallow it, nor suck it. He said: If he does not swallow his saliva, then it is refreshing for him; but if he swallows his saliva while it is said to him that he is forbidden from that, then he has broken fast."
"7296","Ma'mar reported, he said: I heard Qatadah being asked about gum, and he said: I dislike it for the fasting person and for the non-fasting person."
"7297","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, and from Jabir, from al-Shabi: They both disliked gum for the fasting person."
"7298","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about rinsing the mouth for the fasting person outside prayer. He said: I dislike it only because of the saying of Abu Hurayrah; I heard him saying: The breath of the fasting person's mouth is sweeter with Allah than the scent of musk."
"7299","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: There is no harm if the fasting person swallows his saliva."
"7300","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He rinsed his mouth while fasting, then emptied the water - does it harm him to swallow it? He said: It does not harm him; and what remains in his mouth?"
"7301","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A person asked Ata: The fasting person takes sahur, then before prayer finds something in his teeth. He said: There is nothing upon him in that; have you not rinsed your mouth? He said: I said: It was forbidden for the fasting person to rinse at the time of breaking fast and spit it onto the ground before swallowing anything? He said: I dislike that only because of the saying of Abu Hurayrah."
"7302","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: I saw Uthman ibn Abi al-As at Arafah while fasting, spitting out water and pouring water over himself. He said: And al-Hasan used to rinse his mouth while fasting then spit it out, and that in severe heat."
"7303","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, I know only from Umar, he said: When one of you is fasting and breaks fast, let him not rinse then spit it out; rather let him drink it, for its beginning is its best; and let him not wipe his hand with a napkin until he licks it or has it licked."
"7304","From Ma'mar, he said: I was informed that Qatadah rinsed once while fasting at the time of breaking fast, then spat it out, and a man said to him: Is this not disliked? He said: Yes, but I forgot."
"7305","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while at al-Arj, used to pour water over his head while fasting."
"7306","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Sumayy, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in Ramadan on the day of the Conquest fasting; when he reached al-Arj, fasting became hard upon him, so he used to pour water over his head while fasting."
"7307","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked Hammad about the fasting woman tasting broth, and he saw no harm upon her in that. He said: And they say: Nothing is more conclusive in that than water with which the fasting person rinses."
"7308","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: He saw no harm if the fasting woman chews for her child."
"7309","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: I saw him chewing food for a child while fasting. He said: He chews it then takes it out of his mouth, placing it in the child's mouth. Yunus said: And I used to visit him while he was fasting in severe heat, and he would rinse with water, spitting it from noon to afternoon, and that in Rajab."
"7310","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah: He disliked that the fasting person apply aloes as kohl, and saw no harm in antimony."
"7311","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Aloes with which the fasting person applies kohl? He said: Yes, if he wishes."
"7312","From al-Thawri, from al-Qaqa, that he asked Ibrahim about aloes for the fasting person. He said: Apply it as kohl, but do not inhale it as snuff."
"7313","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, and from Layth, from Ata, he said: There is no harm in kohl for the fasting person."
"7314","From Ibn al-Yatmi, that his father, and Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, and Ibn Abi Layla, and Ibn Shubrumah said: If the fasting person applies kohl, upon him is to make up a day in its place. He said: And his father used to dislike kohl for the fasting person."
"7315","From al-Thawri, that he used to dislike kohl for the fasting person. Al-Thawri said: And Wail ibn Dawud informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: Fasting is only from what enters, not from what exits; and ablution is from what exits, not from what enters."
"7316","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Abu Asma al-Rahabi, from Shaddad ibn Aws, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast."
"7317","Ma'mar reported, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani who lived in Syria at Sanaa, from Shaddad ibn Aws, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man being cupped on the eighteenth of Ramadan while I was with him, and said: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast. From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Shaddad ibn Aws similar, and he added, he said: And that was on the day of the Conquest."
"7318","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast."
"7319","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Qariz, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast."
"7320","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Ali, he said: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast."
"7321","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Makhul informed me, that an elder of the tribe informed him, that Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast."
"7322","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken fast."
"7323","From Ma'mar, from Khilad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Shaqiq ibn Thawr, I think from his father, he said: I asked Abu Hurayrah about the fasting person being cupped. He said: They say: The cupper and the cupped have broken fast; yet if I were cupped, I would not care - Abu Hurayrah is the speaker."
"7324","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike cupping for the fasting person only because of weakness."
"7325","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, he said: I asked Abu Hurayrah about a man being cupped while fasting. He said: What if he faints?"
"7326","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar would not be cupped while fasting."
"7327","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: That Ibn Umar used to be cupped while fasting, then abandoned it afterwards; when the sun set, he would be cupped."
"7328","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to be cupped while fasting, then abandoned it afterwards; he would prepare the cupping instruments, and when the sun set, he ordered him to cut."
"7329","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Umar, in Ramadan, would count the cupper, his instruments and his need, until when the fasting person broke fast, he would be cupped at night."
"7330","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a person is cupped in Ramadan - does he make up a day in place of that day? He said: Yes, he has broken fast, and he expiates with what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. I said: What if a person cupped his leg? He said: It is all the same; he has broken fast."
"7331","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade cupping for the fasting person, and continuous fasting, but did not prohibit them out of care for his Companions. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you fast continuously until sahar. He said: I fast continuously until sahar, and my Lord feeds me and gives me drink."
"7332","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while fasting."
"7333","From Ayman ibn Nabil, that he asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: May the fasting person be cupped? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while fasting."
"7334","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man among his companions, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Neither one who vomits, nor one cupped, nor one who has a wet dream breaks fast. He said: And Ma'mar mentioned it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar."
"7335","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: That Sad ibn Abi Waqqas and Aishah saw no harm in it, and they would both be cupped while fasting."
"7336","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while fasting and in ihram, between Makkah and Madinah."
"7337","From al-Thawri, from Furat, from Qays, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That she used to be cupped while fasting."
"7338","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Abd Allah al-Jurmi, from Dinar, he said: I cupped Zayd ibn Arqam while he was fasting."
"7339","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, and Jabir and Ismail, all narrating from al-Shabi, he said: Husayn ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib was cupped while fasting."
"7340","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If he is cupped forgetfully or ignorantly, there is no makeup upon him."
"7341","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: That he used to be cupped while fasting, then would not break fast."
"7342","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A person vomited deliberately, forgetfully or ignorantly? He said: He does not replace that day, and completes it. He said: And Ata said: If a person induces vomiting deliberately in Ramadan, he has broken fast; if he forgets, he has not broken fast. Ibn Jurayj said: Amr ibn Dinar said the same."
"7343","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Yaish ibn al-Walid, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abu al-Darda, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke fast, and water was brought and he performed ablution."
"7344","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He vomited in Ramadan? He said: He makes up that day, and expiates with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said; but if he was forgetful or ignorant."
"7345","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and from Hafs, from al-Hasan, they both said: Whoever induces vomiting has broken fast, and upon him is makeup; whoever is overcome by vomit has not broken fast."
"7346","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whoever induces vomiting has broken fast, and upon him is makeup; whoever is overcome by vomit, there is no makeup upon him."
"7347","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If you vomit or induce vomiting forgetfully, you have not broken fast."
"7348","From Hushaym, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Whoever vomits, upon him is makeup; if vomit overcomes him, there is no makeup upon him. From Hushaym, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah similar."
"7349","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: The pregnant woman in her month, and the nursing woman who fears for her child, both break fast and each feeds a poor person for every day, and there is no makeup upon them. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad informed me, saying: If they cannot fast, let them feed."
"7350","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The pregnant woman who fears for her child breaks fast, and the nursing woman who fears for her child breaks fast, and each of them feeds a poor person for every day, and there is no makeup upon them."
"7351","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The pregnant and the nursing woman break fast in Ramadan when they fear for their children in summer. He said: And in winter, when they fear for their children."
"7352","From Ma'mar and Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Lubaybah, he said: Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Uthman sent me to Ibn Umar, asking him about a woman over whom Ramadan comes while she is pregnant. He said: She breaks fast and feeds a poor person for every day. From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Said similar."
"7353","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Banu Amir: That a man arrived in Madinah and entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a need of his, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was eating. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Come near. He said: I am fasting. Then he said: Come near, for fasting has been lifted from the traveler, and half the prayer, and from the pregnant and the nursing woman."
"7354","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The pregnant woman, when she fears for herself in Ramadan, breaks fast and feeds, and there is no makeup upon her."
"7355","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: She breaks fast and feeds half a sa'."
"7356","From one who heard Ikrimah saying: The pregnant and the nursing woman break fast in Ramadan, and they make up fasting, with no food upon them."
"7357","From al-Thawri and from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The pregnant and the nursing woman break fast in Ramadan, and they make up fasting, and do not feed."
"7358","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: They both make up fasting, in the place of the sick person who breaks fast and makes up; and the nursing woman likewise."
"7359","From Fudayl, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: A woman came to Alqamah and said: I am pregnant, and I can fast, but my husband prevents me. Alqamah said to her: Obey your Lord and disobey your husband."
"7360","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: That he used to order a pregnant slave girl of his to break fast for him in the month of Ramadan, and said: You are in the place of the elder who cannot fast; so break fast, and feed for every day half a sa' of wheat."
"7361","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: From which illness is one to break fast in Ramadan? He said: From all of it. I said: He fasts until when he breaks fast? He said: Yes, as Allah said."
"7362","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked: Does a man have concession to compel his servant to break fast in the month of Ramadan? He said: No. A man said to him: Does the shepherd have concession to break fast? He said: I have not heard of a concession for him. He said: He sees the livestock only a quarter or a third? He said: He should not break fast."
"7363","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: Anas ibn Malik grew old until he could not bear fasting, so he would break fast and feed."
"7364","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Ayyub, from Ikrimah: That they both used to read: And upon those who can bear it, it is imposed though they cannot bear it - they are those who cannot bear, and they break fast. Ma'mar said: And one who heard Said ibn Jubayr and Mujahid informed me, saying that."
"7365","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Ibn Sirin: That Ibn Abbas said concerning this verse: And upon those who can bear it, a ransom of feeding a poor person - no other verse abrogated it: So whoever witnesses the month, let him fast it."
"7366","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I heard Ikrimah narrating from Ibn Abbas: That it is not abrogated; he used to read it: yutawwaqunahu - it is concerning the elder who is burdened with fasting and cannot bear it, so he breaks fast and feeds."
"7367","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: That he used to read it: And upon those who are burdened with it, and says: It is the elderly man who cannot fast, so he breaks fast and feeds for every day a poor person, half a sa' of wheat."
"7368","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: And upon those who can bear it, a ransom of feeding a poor person. He said: Ibn Abbas used to read it: yutawwaqunahu. Ata said: And it reached me that the elder, when he cannot fast, ransoms for every day of Ramadan with a mudd for each poor person - the elderly man and the elderly woman. As for one who can fast it with effort, let him fast it; there is no excuse for him in leaving it. I said: What if an elder unable to fast the month of Ramadan leaves it, and does not give charity until another Ramadan month overtakes him? He said: He gives charity another time as makeup for what he left and for what overtook him afterwards - he does not give another charity for what he left; that is only upon one upon whom fasting is due, then he neglects it, that he make it up until he makes up the other."
"7369","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Abi Jafar informed me, from Abu Amr, freedman of Aishah: That Aishah used to read: wa yutawwaqunahu."
"7370","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: That he used to read: And upon those who are burdened with it - and it is the aged man and the aged woman who cannot fast; they break fast and feed for every day a poor person, each of them."
"7371","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: His saying: And upon those who can bear it was abrogated by: So whoever witnesses the month, let him fast it."
"7372","From Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: He used to read: And upon those who can bear it. He said: It is concerning the elderly man and the old woman, when they cannot fast; upon them is to feed a poor person for every day, each of them; if they find nothing, nothing is upon them."
"7373","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: He feeds for every day a poor person, a makkuk of wheat, a makkuk of dates."
"7374","From Ikrimah ibn Ammar, he said: I asked Tawus about my mother - she had thirst and could not fast Ramadan. He said: She feeds for every day a poor person, a mudd of wheat. He said: I said: With which mudd? He said: The mudd of your land."
"7375","From Ma'mar, from his father, and al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: So whoever volunteers good, they said: He feeds another poor person. And Ibn Jurayj said it, from Mujahid."
"7376","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is yutiqunahu? He said: They are burdened with it. And Ibn Jubayr said it. He said: So he ransoms for every day of Ramadan with a mudd for each poor person; So whoever volunteers good - whoever adds over feeding a poor person."
"7377","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning His saying: And upon those who can bear it, he said: It was concerning the elderly man and the elderly woman who cannot fast, and it is hard upon them; so they were permitted to break fast, then that was abrogated afterwards, and He said: So whoever witnesses the month, let him fast it."
"7378","From al-Aslami, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: It is concerning the elderly man, when he cannot fast; he ransoms in place of every day, feeding a poor person a mudd of wheat."
"7379","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you breaks fast, let him break fast with dates; if he does not find, let him break fast with water, for water is purifying. From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar."
"7380","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: That Umar and Uthman used to pray Maghrib in Ramadan before breaking fast."
"7381","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from his father, he said: I was sitting with Umar when a party from Syria came to him, and Umar began asking about their condition. He said: Do the people of Syria hasten breaking fast? He said: Yes. He said: They will remain in good as long as they do that, and do not await the stars like the people of Iraq await."
"7382","From al-Thawri, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to the governors of the lands: Do not be of those who delay your breaking fast, nor those who await for your prayer the intertwining of the stars."
"7383","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were the quickest of people to break fast and the slowest to take sahur."
"7384","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People remain in good as long as they hasten breaking fast."
"7385","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur or Layth, from Mujahid, he said: I would bring Ibn Umar a cup at his breaking fast, hiding it from people; there was nothing with him but shyness - he says: from how quickly he breaks fast."
"7386","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Shaybani, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he said to a man of the people: Dismount and mix something for me - while he was fasting. He said: The sun, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Dismount and mix for me. He said: So he dismounted and mixed for him, and he drank. And he said: If one of you saw it upon his camel, he would see it - meaning the sun. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed with his hand toward the east, he said: When you see the night approaching from here, the fasting person has broken fast."
"7387","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Asim ibn Umar, he said: Umar said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the night approaches and the day retreats, and the sun sets, the fasting person has broken fast."
"7388","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Urwah ibn Iyad informing Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah that he is commanded for a person to break fast before praying, even upon a sip."
"7389","From a companion of his, from Awf, from Abu Raja, he said: I used to witness Ibn Abbas at breaking fast in Ramadan; his food would be placed, then he would order a watcher to watch the sun; when he said: It has set, he said: Eat. He said: Then we would break fast before prayer."
"7390","Ma'mar reported, from Abd al-Aziz, freedman of Anas, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take sahur, for in sahur is blessing."
"7391","Ma'mar reported, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu al-Walid Abd Allah ibn al-Harith al-Ansari: That a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take sahur, even with a sip of water."
"7392","From Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Madan: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come to the pleasant blessed morning meal - meaning sahur."
"7393","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take sahur, for in sahur is blessing."
"7394","From al-Thawri, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from a man called Musa ibn Ali, from his father, from a freedman of Amr ibn al-As called Abu Qays, from Amr ibn al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The difference between our fast and the fast of the People of the Book is the meal of sahar."
"7395","From Shaybah ibn al-Numan ibn Ismail ibn Sharus, that he heard Ismail saying: I heard Tawus, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek help by the sleep of the day for the standing of the night, and by the meal of sahar for the fast of the day."
"7396","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, he said: al-Walid ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Mughith informed me, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What an excellent aid is the daytime nap for standing in prayer at night."
"7397","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give me sahur to eat, O Anas! I intend to fast, so feed me something. So I brought him dates and a vessel containing water after Bilal had given the adhan. He said: O Anas, look for a person to eat with me. So I called Zayd ibn Thabit, and he said: O Messenger of Allah! I drank a drink of sawiq, and I intend to fast. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I intend to fast, so he ate sahur with him. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rak'as, then went out and the prayer was established. And Ma'mar used to delay the sahur and pray at spreading daylight, until the ignorant would say: He has no fast."
"7398","From Isra'il, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salama, he said: I set out, I and Zirr ibn Hubaysh, to Hudhayfa while he was in the house of al-Harith ibn Abi Rabi'a. We sought permission to see him, and he came out to us, and he brought milk and said: Drink. We said: We intend to fast. He said: And I intend to fast. So he drank, then handed it to Zirr and he drank, then handed it to me and I drank, while the mu'adhdhin was giving the adhan in the mosque. He said: When we entered the mosque the prayer was established, and they were praying in the darkness before dawn."
"7399","From his father Hammam, he said: al-Muntashir al-Wadi'i narrated to me that Umayr Dhabitan informed him that he ate sahur with Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas at Kufa in Ramadan, then went out, I being with him, and came to the mosque and the prayer was established. He said: I said: How far was between his house and the mosque? He said: The distance between the grave of Ziyad ibn Fayruz and the great mosque."
"7400","From Ibn Uyayna, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Hakim ibn Jabir, he said: Bilal came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was eating sahur. He said: Prayer, O Messenger of Allah! So he remained as he was, eating. Then he came to him and said: Prayer, while he was in the same state. Then he came to him a third time and said: Prayer, O Messenger of Allah! By Allah, it is morning. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on Bilal; were it not for Bilal we would have hoped that concession would be granted to us until the sun rises."
"7401","From Ibn Uyayna, from Shabib ibn Gharqada, from Hibban ibn al-Harith, he said: I came to Ali while he was encamped at Dayr Abi Musa and he was eating sahur. He said: Come near. He said: I said: I intend to fast. He said: And I intend to fast. When he finished, he said to the mu'adhdhin: Establish the prayer."
"7402","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Hazim, freedman of the Ansar, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Part of seventy parts of prophethood is delaying the sahur, hastening the iftar, and a man's pointing with his finger in prayer."
"7403","Ubayd Allah ibn Umar reported from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal gives the adhan at night, so whoever intends to fast should eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the adhan. He said: And al-Qasim said: And there was between the two only that this one descends and this one ascends. From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah, from al-Qasim, the like of it."
"7404","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal gives the adhan at night, so whoever intends to fast, the adhan of Bilal should not prevent him, until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the adhan. He said: And he was blind, and he would not give the adhan until it was said to him: It is morning."
"7405","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal gives the adhan at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the adhan."
"7406","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one of the people of knowledge informed me: Among the character of the prophets, upon them be peace: hastening the iftar, delaying the sahur, and placing the right hand over the left in prayer."
"7407","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, freedman of the family of Ali, that some people of Thaqif came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he lodged them at the graveyard, and that was in Ramadan. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent them their sahur after the adhan of Bilal, after the rising of the first dawn, when it had become quite bright. So they ate, and Bilal ate with them, then they all fasted. Then Bilal sent them their iftar when they thought the sun had set, while they were in doubt, so they broke fast and he broke fast with them."
"7408","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sa'id ibn Jahman, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Qatada on an errand of his. He came to him after it had become quite bright, meaning after the first dawn. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) presented sahur to him. He said: O Messenger of Allah! It is morning. He said: Take sahur. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept closing the door so that the brightness would not show to him. When he finished, he went out and found it had become quite bright, meaning after the first dawn."
"7409","From Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, that Abu Bakr used to say: Close the door, lest the morning surprise us."
"7410","From Abu Sufyan, from Mis'ar, from Jabala ibn Suhaym, from Amir ibn Matar al-Shaybani, from his father, he said: We ate sahur with Abd Allah, then we went out and the prayer was established."
"7411","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Mujahid, from Abu Hurayra, he said: Whoever is overtaken by Ramadan while sick, then recovers but does not make it up until another Ramadan overtakes him, he fasts the one he has met, then fasts the first one, and feeds for each day half a sa' of wheat. Ma'mar said: And I do not know but all of them say this about this."
"7412","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Abu Hurayra, he said: If a person fell sick in Ramadan, then recovered, but did not make it up until another month of Ramadan overtook him, then let him fast the current one, then make up the other, and feed a poor person with each day."
"7413","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: He feeds in place of the month that passed, because he recovered but was negligent in making it up until another month of Ramadan overtook him. I said to Ata: How much has it reached you that he feeds? He said: A mudd, as they claimed."
"7414","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Whoever is followed by another Ramadan while sick, not having recovered between the two, he makes up the later of the two by fasting, and makes up the earlier of the two by feeding a mudd of wheat, and he does not fast it."
"7415","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whoever fell sick in Ramadan and is overtaken by another Ramadan while sick, and did not fast this later one, he does not fast the first, and he feeds for each day of the first Ramadan a mudd. He said: And that reached me from Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"7416","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: Whoever is followed by two Ramadans while sick, not having recovered between them, he makes up this later one of the two by fasting, and makes up the earlier one of the two by food, and he does not fast it."
"7417","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He makes them both up by fasting. From Ma'mar, and I heard Hammad saying the like of the saying of Tawus."
"7418","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, from Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Abbas when a man came to him and said: Two Ramadans have consecutively passed over me. Ibn Abbas said: By Allah, did this happen? He said: Yes. He said: No. Then he went away, then another came and said: A man has had two Ramadans consecutively pass over him. He said: By Allah, did this happen? He said: Yes. Ibn Abbas said: One of seven: he fasts two months and feeds sixty poor persons."
"7419","From a shaykh of the people of al-Jazira, he said: I heard Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj saying: We went out on a detachment in the land of the Romans, and while we were in the land of the Romans, with us was Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i. He addressed us, and I heard him saying: I heard Umar, Commander of the Believers, saying: Whoever fasts a day other than Ramadan, and feeds a poor person, and joins them in his two hands, then they equal a day of Ramadan."
"7420","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: About a man sick in Ramadan who remains sick until he dies, he said: There is nothing upon him; but if he recovers and does not fast until he dies, there is fed on his behalf for each day half a sa' of wheat. From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, the like of the saying of Ibn Abbas."
"7421","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: When a man falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, there is nothing upon him; but if he recovers and does not make it up until he dies, there is fed on his behalf for each day a makkuuk of wheat and a makkuuk of dates."
"7422","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When a man falls sick in Ramadan and does not recover until he dies, there is nothing upon him; he was overcome in his affair and his making up."
"7423","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a man falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, there is nothing upon him; but if he recovers and does not make it up, there is fed on his behalf for each day a poor person a mudd of wheat."
"7424","From al-Aslami, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Ubada ibn Nusayy, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, nothing is fed on his behalf; but if he recovers and does not make it up until he dies, food is fed on his behalf."
"7425","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When a man falls sick in Ramadan and remains sick until he dies, there is fed on his behalf in place of each day for a poor person a mudd of wheat. From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: Food is fed on his behalf. From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I mentioned to Ibn Sirin the saying of Tawus, and it did not please him."
"7426","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He fell sick in Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up, until Ramadan passed over him three times while he was healthy? He said: He feeds once: thirty poor persons thirty mudds."
"7427","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man fell sick the whole of Ramadan and remained sick until another Ramadan passed over him? He said: He feeds once only. I said to him: A man fell sick the whole of Ramadan and remained sick until the other overtook him while sick? He said: He makes up the first only, and does not feed."
"7428","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man fell sick in Ramadan until another Ramadan overtook him while sick, and he was sick the whole of it, then recovered, but did not make up both, until the third overtook him, how much does he feed? He said: Sixty poor persons sixty mudds."
"7429","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man fell sick the whole of Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up until he died, he said: There is fed on his behalf thirty poor persons thirty mudds. I said: A man fell sick the whole of Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up until another Ramadan overtook him, then he died in it or after it, he said: There is fed on his behalf sixty poor persons sixty mudds."
"7430","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, about a man who fell sick the whole of Ramadan, then recovered but did not make it up until another Ramadan overtook him, then he died in it or after it, he said: There is fed on his behalf in place of the first, for each day, two poor persons, as he did."
"7431","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When a man dies owing fasting of another Ramadan, there is fed on his behalf for each day half a sa' of wheat."
"7432","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Ata, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, he said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! My mother died owing the fast of a month. He said: Fast in her place."
"7433","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When a man dies owing fasting of Ramadan, one of his guardians fasts on his behalf. Ma'mar said: And Hammad said it."
"7434","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, that a woman died owing the fast of a year, leaving her husband and her three sons. Tawus said: All of you fast a year on her behalf."
"7435","From al-Zuhri, about a man who died owing a vowed fast which he did not make up, he said: One of his guardians fasts on his behalf."
"7436","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, and Ibn Jurayj from Ata, they said: There is fed on his behalf for each day a poor person."
"7437","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban al-Ansari, from Ibn Abbas, about a man who died owing Ramadan and owing a vowed fast of another month, he said: Sixty poor persons are fed on his behalf."
"7438","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that it reached him from Ibn Abbas that he said: There is fed on his behalf in place of Ramadan for each day a poor person, and one of his guardians fasts the vow on his behalf. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Uthman ibn Matar mentioned it from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Ali ibn al-Hakam, from Ibn Abbas."
"7439","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, about the two months overtaking the traveler, he said: Like the sick person alike. I said: A man broke fast some days of Ramadan while traveling, then died on that journey before settling? He said: There is nothing upon him, nor is food fed on his behalf."
"7440","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man broke fast in Ramadan while traveling, then remained traveling until another Ramadan overtook him while traveling, all between that? He said: There is nothing upon him except that he makes up the first, and he does not have to feed. I said: A man broke the fast of Ramadan while traveling, then settled but did not make it up until the next Ramadan met him while traveling, may he break fast if he wishes? He said: Yes, then he feeds thirty poor persons thirty mudds."
"7441","From al-Thawri, about a man who breaks fast some days while traveling, then dies on his journey, he said: There is nothing upon him, and it enters into the saying of Ibn Abbas, al-Nakha'i, al-Hasan, Ata, and al-Zuhri."
"7442","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, about a man who breaks fast some days while traveling, then dies before settling, he said: There is fed on his behalf for each day a poor person."
"7443","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said, about the making up of Ramadan: Fast it as you broke it."
"7444","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Fast it as you broke it. He said: And Urwa said: A'isha said: It was revealed: A number of other days in succession, then the word in succession fell."
"7445","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: He makes it up consecutively."
"7446","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, and from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, they said: Consecutively."
"7447","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Consecutively."
"7448","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Consecutively."
"7449","He said: Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Fast it as you wish, and count the number."
"7450","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, he used to prefer it consecutively."
"7451","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayra said about Ramadan: Separate it when you have counted it."
"7452","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Fast as you wish. Allah said: A number of other days."
"7453","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: If he wishes, he separates."
"7454","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Fast as you wish when you have counted its fasting."
"7455","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Ibn Muhayriz, he said: Count the number, and fast as you wish. From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilaba, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhayriz, the like of it."
"7456","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Mujahid, he said: If you wish, separate; it is only a number of other days."
"7457","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrima, he was asked about the making up of Ramadan, together or separate? He said: Whichever of that he wishes. Allah said: Two consecutive months; and had He willed He would have said: Whoever makes up Ramadan, then together. But He did not say anything about it, and the righteous people did not forbid it, for they follow what is lawful."
"7458","From al-Thawri, from a Qurashi man, from his mother, that she asked Abu Hurayra about the making up of Ramadan, and he said: There is no harm in separating it; it is only a number of other days."
"7459","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Hisham ibn Yahya, from Abu Hurayra, he said: Fast as you wish, and count the number. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Hurayra."
"7460","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: What do you think, if a man owes some days of Ramadan, and he wakes in the morning with no intention of fasting, then it occurs to him after morning, may he count it as making up Ramadan, though he did not obligate it before dawn? He said: Let him fast it, and count it as making up Ramadan."
"7461","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: I was with Ibn al-Musayyib when a bedouin came to him at Asr or after Asr and said: I have not eaten anything today, shall I fast? He said: Yes. He said: I owe a day of Ramadan, shall I count it in its place? He said: Yes."
"7462","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me that he heard Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I heard A'isha saying: Something of Ramadan would be due upon me, then I would be unable to fast it until Sha'ban comes. He said: I thought that was due to her place with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Yahya says it."
"7463","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Salama, from A'isha, she said: Days of Ramadan would be due upon me, and I would not make them up except in Sha'ban."
"7464","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata used to say: He grants him respite as long as another Ramadan does not overtake him."
"7465","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, and Asim, that they heard Ikrima saying: Ibn Abbas said: Umar ibn al-Khattab called the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked them about Laylat al-Qadr, and they agreed it is in the last ten. Ibn Abbas said: So I said to Umar: I know, or I think I know, which night it is? Umar said: And which night is it? I said: The seventh passing, or the seventh remaining, of the last ten. Umar said: And whence did you know that? He said: Allah created seven heavens, and seven earths, and seven days, and time revolves in seven, and Allah created man from seven, and he eats from seven, and prostrates upon seven, and circumambulation of the House is seven, and stoning of the jamarat is seven, for things he mentioned. Umar said: You have indeed perceived a matter we did not perceive. And Qatada used to add to Ibn Abbas in his saying: He eats from seven, he said: It is the saying of Allah: Then We caused to grow therein grain and grapes."
"7466","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that a man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I saw Laylat al-Qadr as if it were such-and-such night. He said: I see your dreams have coincided upon the last ten, so seek it in nine, in odd."
"7467","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the ten that remain, in the nine that remain, in odd."
"7468","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, that they heard Ibn Shihab narrating from Urwa, from A'isha, and from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to observe i'tikaf in the last ten of Ramadan until Allah took him."
"7469","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) observed i'tikaf in the first ten of Ramadan, and it was said to him: What you seek is ahead of you. So he observed i'tikaf in the middle ten of Ramadan, and it was said to him: What you seek is ahead of you. So he observed i'tikaf in the last ten, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek it in the last ten in odd, meaning Laylat al-Qadr. From al-Thawri, from Abu Harun, from Abu Sa'id, the like of it, except that he said: He observed i'tikaf, and we observed i'tikaf with him."
"7470","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: We were discussing Laylat al-Qadr among a group of Quraysh, so I went to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and he was a friend of mine, and I said: Will you not go out with us to the date-palms? He said: Yes. He said: So he went out wearing a khamisa of his. He said: So I said to him: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning Laylat al-Qadr? He said: Yes; we observed i'tikaf with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the middle ten of the month of Ramadan, and we came out on the morning of the twentieth. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: I saw Laylat al-Qadr, then I was made to forget it, so seek it in the last ten, in odd. And I saw myself prostrating in water and mud, so whoever observed i'tikaf with me, let him return to his place of i'tikaf. He said: So we returned, and there was not a cloud-piece in the sky. Then a cloud came and we were rained upon, until the roof of the mosque flowed, and it was of palm branches, and the prayer was established. So I saw upon the nose of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he turned away, the trace of mud on his forehead and his nose, meaning the night of the twenty-first."
"7471","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, he said: Ibn Abbas used to sprinkle water upon his family on the night of the twenty-third."
"7472","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yunus ibn Sayf informed me that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among a group of his companions and said: Shall I not inform you of Laylat al-Qadr? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah! Then he was silent for a while, then said: I said to you what I said just now while I knew it, and I indeed know it, then I was made to forget it. Do you recall a day when we were at such-and-such place, on which night it is? In a campaign he campaigned. They said: We marched and did so, until the assembly of the people settled upon that it is the night of the twenty-third."
"7473","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! I saw in sleep Laylat al-Qadr as if it were a seventh night. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I see your dreams have coincided about a seventh night, so whoever of you seeks it, let him seek it on a seventh night. Ma'mar said: And Ayyub used to bathe on the night of the twenty-third and touch perfume."
"7474","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub and others, from some of them, that al-Juhani came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! I am a man of the desert and livestock, so advise me of Laylat al-Qadr in which I may stand. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Or two nights? He said: Rather one night. So he called him and spoke to him privately, no one knowing what he commanded him. The people said: Look at the night in which al-Juhani stands. So when it was the night of the twenty-third, he would come down with his family and stand that night."
"7475","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that al-Juhani, Abd Allah ibn Unays, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah! I am burdened with dependents and lands, and he was a man of crops, so command me a night. He said: Or two nights? He said: Rather one night. So he called him and spoke to him privately two or three times, and commanded him the night of the twenty-third. So he would spend that evening in the mosque, and not leave it until morning, and would not attend anything of Ramadan before it nor after it, nor the day of Fitr."
"7476","From Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, that Abd Allah ibn Unays al-Juhani said: O Messenger of Allah! I am a man far from home, so command me a night in which I may come down. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come down on the night of the twenty-third."
"7477","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Isa ibn Abd Allah ibn Unays, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him the night of the twenty-third."
"7478","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul: That he used to consider it the night of the twenty-third. Al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to him from Abda ibn Abi Lubaba that he said: The night of the twenty-seventh, and that he had tested that by things and by the stars. But Makhul paid no heed to that."
"7479","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Atiyya ibn Abd Allah ibn Unays, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to come down to Madina on the night of the twenty-third of Ramadan."
"7480","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: A'isha used to wake us on the night of the twenty-third of Ramadan."
"7481","And Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me from his father that Ali used to seek Laylat al-Qadr on the night of the nineteenth, the twenty-first, and the twenty-third."
"7482","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr on the night of the seventeenth, the morning of Badr, or the twenty-first, or the twenty-third."
"7483","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: I observed the sun for twenty years, and I saw it rise on the morning of the twenty-fourth of Ramadan without rays."
"7484","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: Laylat al-Qadr moves in the last ten in odd."
"7485","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I said: O Abu al-Mundhir! Meaning Ubayy ibn Ka'b, inform me about Laylat al-Qadr, for Ibn Umm Abd says: Whoever stands the year attains it. He said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Abd al-Rahman; he knew it is in Ramadan, but he concealed it from people lest they rely. By the One Who sent down the Book upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is indeed in the month of Ramadan, and it is indeed the night of the twenty-seventh. He said: I said: O Abu al-Mundhir! By what did you know that? He said: By the sign which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us of, and we saw and memorized; by Allah, it is indeed it, without exception. He said: I said to Zirr: And what is the sign? He said: That the sun rises that morning as if it were a basin without rays."
"7486","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Sharik, he said: I saw Zirr ibn Hubaysh, while al-Hajjaj stood upon the pulpit mentioning Laylat al-Qadr, and it was as if he said: Some people mention Laylat al-Qadr. Zirr kept wanting to leap upon him while the people restrained him. Zirr said: It is the night of the twenty-seventh; whoever attains it, let him bathe, and break fast upon milk, and let his breaking of fast be at sahar."
"7487","From al-Thawri, from some of his companions, from A'isha, she said: When the last ten of Ramadan entered, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake his family and tighten the waist-wrapper. Sufyan says: Tightening the waist-wrapper: not approaching women."
"7488","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayra ibn Yarim, from Ali: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake his family in the last ten of Ramadan."
"7489","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Ya'fur, from Muslim, from Masruq, from A'isha, she said: When the last ten of Ramadan entered, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake his family, enliven his night, and tighten the waist-wrapper."
"7490","From Hushaym, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, that he used to complete the Qur'an in the month of Ramadan every three days; and when the ten entered, he completed in two nights, and bathed every night."
"7491","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami, from Abu Dharr, he said: We fasted Ramadan with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not lead us in prayer any of the month, until seven remained. Then he led us until about a third of the night had gone. Then he did not lead us the fourth night, and he led us the night following it, until about half the night had gone. He said: So we said: O Messenger of Allah! Would that you supererogate for us the rest of this night of ours. He said: When a man stands with the imam until he turns away, the rest of his night is counted for him. Then he did not lead us the sixth, and he led us the seventh, and he sent for his family, and the people gathered. So he led us until we feared al-falah would escape us. He said: I said: And what is al-falah? He said: The sahur."
"7492","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me from Abd Allah ibn Yahnas, he said: I said to Abu Hurayra: They claim that Laylat al-Qadr has been lifted. He said: He who said that lied. He said: I said: Is it in every Ramadan I meet? He said: Yes."
"7493","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, Ibn Abbas said: Laylat al-Qadr comes in every Ramadan. He said: And Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hadd narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Laylat al-Qadr? It was said to him: It was with the prophets, then it was lifted when they passed away, or is it in every year? He said: Rather it is in every year, rather it is in every year."
"7494","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that a shaykh of the people of Madina asked Abu Dharr at Mina, saying: Has Laylat al-Qadr been lifted, or is it in every Ramadan? Abu Dharr said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah! Has Laylat al-Qadr been lifted? He said: Rather it is in every Ramadan."
"7495","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, it was disliked to make up Ramadan in the ten. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan saying it informed me."
"7496","From Hisham ibn Hassan, that he disliked making up Ramadan in the ten."
"7497","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Murra, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Ramadan is not made up in Dhu al-Hijja."
"7498","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim and Sa'id ibn Jubayr about a man owing days of Ramadan, may he voluntary-fast in the ten? They said: He begins with the obligation."
"7499","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, that Umar used to prefer that Ramadan be made up in the ten."
"7500","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn Mawhab, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra, a man asking him, saying: I owe days of Ramadan, shall I fast the ten voluntarily? He said: No, and why? Begin with the right of Allah, then voluntary-fast whatever you wish after."
"7501","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: From Ata: It was disliked that a man voluntary-fast in the ten while obligatory fasting is upon him. He said: No, but fast the ten, and count it as making up."
"7502","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hayyan, from an old woman, from A'isha, she said: No, rather until you discharge the right."
"7503","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: Whoever owes fasting of Ramadan and voluntary-fasts, let him count what he voluntary-fasted as making up Ramadan."
"7504","From Ma'mar, and Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to encourage standing in Ramadan, without commanding them decisively, and would say: Whoever stands Ramadan in faith and seeking reward, forgiven for him is what preceded of his sin. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away with the matter upon that, then the matter remained likewise in the caliphate of Abu Bakr, and the beginning of the caliphate of Umar upon that."
"7505","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever stands Ramadan in faith and seeking reward, forgiven for him is what preceded of his sin."
"7506","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led the people in prayer three nights remaining of Ramadan."
"7507","From Muhammad ibn Umara, he said: Abu Umayya al-Thaqafi informed me from Arfaja that Ali used to command the people to stand in Ramadan, appointing an imam for the men and an imam for the women. He said: So he commanded me and I led the women."
"7508","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, and he used to work for Umar with Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam upon the treasury, he said: Umar went out one night, with him Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and that was in Ramadan, while the people were scattered groups: a man praying by himself, and a man praying with a group praying by his prayer. Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I think that if we gathered these upon one reciter it would be better. So he resolved to gather them upon one reciter, and commanded Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and he led them. Then he went out one night while the people were praying by the prayer of their reciter, and said: What an excellent innovation this is; and that which they sleep from is better than that which they stand, meaning the end of the night. And they used to stand in the beginning of the night."
"7509","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Ubayy used to lead the people in the time of Umar in Ramadan; and when it was the half, he raised voice with qunut after the rak'a. When twenty nights completed, he turned to his family, and Abu Halima Mu'adh the reciter led the people, raising voice with qunut in the last ten, until, from what they heard him, they would say: O Allah, rain has been withheld. They would say: Amen. He would say: How quickly you say Amen; leave me until I supplicate."
"7510","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to perform witr with three, not saluting except in the third, a witr like Maghrib."
"7511","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Hands were not raised in the witr in Ramadan."
"7512","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Imran ibn Musa informed me that Yazid ibn Khusayfa informed them from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Umar, he said: The people were gathered upon Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Tamim al-Dari. Ubayy would perform witr with three rak'as."
"7513","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Umar was the first to perform qunut in Ramadan, in the latter half of Ramadan, between the rak'a and the prostration."
"7514","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to perform qunut in the latter half of Ramadan after bowing."
"7515","Ma'mar said: One who heard Ibrahim saying informed me: Ibn Mas'ud used to perform qunut the whole year."
"7516","From Dawud ibn Qays and others, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, that Umar gathered the people in Ramadan upon Ubayy ibn Ka'b and upon Tamim al-Dari, upon twenty-one rak'as; they would recite of the hundreds and turn away at the branches of dawn."
"7517","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from al-Hasan, he said: They used to recite with thirty-nine, or forty-one. He said: And the people at Makka in the time of Umar and others would fast and circumambulate, until al-Qasri gathered them."
"7518","From al-Thawri, from al-Qasim, from Abu Uthman, he said: Umar commanded three reciters to recite in Ramadan. He commanded the fastest of them to recite thirty verses, commanded the middle of them to recite twenty-five, and commanded the lowest of them to recite twenty. Al-Thawri said: And the reciters would gather in three in Ramadan."
"7519","From al-Aslami, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhubab, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, he said: We used to turn away from the standing in the time of Umar when the branches of dawn had drawn near. And the standing in the time of Umar was twenty-three rak'as."
"7520","From Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, he said: I heard him saying: I did not meet the people except that they cursed the disbelievers in the month of Ramadan. He said: The reciters would stand with Surat al-Baqara in eight rak'as; and when the reciters stood with it in twelve rak'as, the people considered that he had lightened for them."
"7521","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that the standing in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan: a group and a man would stand, likewise here and a group behind a man. Umar was the first to gather the people upon one reciter. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: Umar gathered them upon one reciter."
"7522","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Umar did not gather the people of Makka upon one reciter because of circumambulation; he left whoever wished to circumambulate."
"7523","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that one of their rulers, Mu'awiya or other than him, wanted to gather the people of Makka upon one reciter. Mukram Karnbas said: Do not do it; leave the people: whoever wishes circumambulates, and whoever wishes prays by the prayer of the reciter. So he did."
"7524","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the first to lead the people of Makka in the caliphate of Umar ibn al-Khattab was Zayd ibn Qunfudh ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an. Whoever wished stood with him, whoever wished stood by himself, and whoever wished circumambulated."
"7525","From Ma'mar, from Matar, from al-Hasan, he said: The people used to stand in Ramadan, praying Isha when a quarter of the night had gone, and turning away with another quarter remaining upon them."
"7526","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: Umar called me to eat sahur with him, and to eat midday meal in the month of Ramadan. Umar heard the commotion of the people when they came out of the mosque and said: What is this? I said: The people when they came out of the mosque. He said: What remains of the night is more beloved to me than what has gone."
"7527","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: Abd Allah used to lead us in prayer in the month of Ramadan, turning away at night."
"7528","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: A man came to Ibn Umar and said: Shall I pray behind the imam in Ramadan? He said: Do you recite the Qur'an? He said: Yes. He said: Will you listen silently as if you were a donkey? Pray in your house."
"7529","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he would not stand behind the imam in Ramadan."
"7530","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hamza, from Ibrahim, he said: Even if I had only two suras with me, I would repeat them; more beloved to me."
"7531","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: They saw no harm in a man praying alone in the rear of the mosque in Ramadan while the imam prays."
"7532","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from A'isha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one night in the month of Ramadan in the mosque, with people with him. Then he prayed the second, and there gathered that night more than the first. When it was the third or the fourth, the mosque filled until it was crowded with its people, and he did not come out to them. The people began calling to him: Prayer. When morning came, Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The people kept waiting for you last night, O Messenger of Allah! He said: It was not hidden from me, their affair, but I feared it would be written upon them."
"7533","Ma'mar reported, and Ibn Jurayj, they said: Ibn Shihab informed us from Urwa, from A'isha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one night in the middle of the night and prayed in the mosque. Men spent the night and prayed with him by his prayer. When the people awoke, they talked that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and prayed in the mosque. So the people gathered until the mosque almost could not contain its people. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and did not come out to them, until I heard people saying: Prayer. But he did not come out. When he prayed Fajr, he saluted, then stood among the people, testified, then said: To proceed! Your affair last night was not hidden from me, but I feared it would be made obligatory upon you and you would be unable for it."
"7534","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Khilad, from Abd Allah ibn Ukaym al-Juhani, and he had met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the first night of Ramadan entered, Umar ibn al-Khattab would pray Maghrib, then say: Sit. Then he walked through a light sermon, saying: To proceed! This month, its fasting has been written upon you, and its standing has not been written upon you. Whoever of you is able to stand, let him stand, for they are the supererogatory good which Allah mentioned. Whoever is unable, let him sleep upon his bed. And let each of you beware saying: I fast if so-and-so fasts, and I stand if so-and-so stands. Whoever of you fasts or stands, let him make that for Allah. And let each of you know that he is in prayer as long as he awaits prayer. Lessen idle talk in the houses of Allah, two or three times. Then he would say: None of you shall precede the month, three times. And do not fast until you see it, or they fast until they see it, except that it is clouded over you; and if it is clouded over you, then count thirty. Then do not break fast until you see the night darkening upon the mountain-tops."
"7535","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abd al-Malik, he said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to lead us in the month of Ramadan. He would recite with both recitations together: one night reciting the recitation of Ibn Mas'ud. He would pray five tarwihat; and when it was the last ten, he prayed six tarwihat."
"7536","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, they said: When a man is praying between two tarwihat in Ramadan, and the imam says takbir before he bows, there is no harm that he completes his prayer by the prayer of the imam without bowing."
"7537","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform wisal from sahar to sahar."
"7538","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Abd al-A'la, from Muhammad ibn Ali, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform wisal from sahar to sahar."
"7539","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not perform wisal. They said: O Messenger of Allah! But you perform wisal. He said: I am not like you; I spend night, my Lord feeds me and gives me drink. He said: But they did not desist from wisal. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wisal with them for two days and two nights. Then they saw the crescent. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had the crescent been delayed, I would have increased you, as a rebuke to them."
"7540","From Ma'mar, from Hammam, that he heard Abu Hurayra saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Beware of wisal, beware of wisal. They said: But you perform wisal. He said: In that, I am not like you; I remain, my Lord feeds me and gives me drink. So undertake of deeds what you have capacity for."
"7541","From Ma'mar, from Abu 'Amr al-Nadbi, he is Nubayh al-'Anazi, who said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not practice wisal (continuous fasting without breaking the fast)."" They said: ""But you practice wisal."" He said: ""I am not like you; I spend the night being fed and given drink."""
"7542","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wisal. They said: ""But you practice wisal."" He said: ""How do you know? Perhaps my Lord feeds me and gives me drink."" Ibn Jurayj said: And I heard 'Ata say something similar. He said: And Tawus used to say: ""Wisal was forbidden."""
"7543","From Ma'mar, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, from 'Ali: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""No muwasalah (continuous unbroken fasting)."""
"7544","From Ma'mar, from Haram ibn 'Uthman, from 'Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad, the two sons of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, from their father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""No muwasalah in fasting."""
"7545","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from 'Abidah al-Salmani, who said: Whoever travels in Ramadan after having fasted its beginning as a resident, let him fast its end. Have you not heard that Allah says: ""So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it."""
"7546","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, who said: When Ramadan begins for a man among his family and he fasts some days of it, then travels, if he wishes he fasts and if he wishes he breaks his fast. And Ibn Jurayj said it from 'Ata."
"7547","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: 'Ali said: I do not regard fasting as anything but obligatory upon him. Allah says: ""So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it."""
"7548","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out for Makkah in the year of the Conquest during Ramadan until he reached al-Kudayd, then he broke his fast; so breaking the fast was the later of the two rulings."
"7549","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, who said: 'Asim ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Asim informed me, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered a man who had fasted Ramadan while traveling to make it up. And 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me of it from Kulthum ibn Jabr, from 'Umar."
"7550","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub: that Umm Dharr visited 'A'ishah to greet her, and that was in Ramadan, and 'A'ishah said to her: ""Will you travel in Ramadan? I do not like to travel in Ramadan; and if it caught up with me while I was traveling, I would take up residence."""
"7551","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata: ""Fasting while traveling?"" He said: ""Break your fast when you shorten (the prayer), and fast when you pray the full prayer."""
"7552","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: When a man wakes up fasting in the month of Ramadan, then sets out traveling during the day, he should not break his fast that day except if he fears thirst for himself; if he fears it, he breaks his fast, and making up is upon him. Thereafter, if he wishes he breaks his fast, and if he wishes he fasts. Ma'mar said: And Jabir al-Ju'fi informed me from al-Sha'bi, who said: ""When he sets out during the day in Ramadan, he may break his fast as soon as he sets out, if he wishes."""
"7553","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata informed me that Ibn 'Abbas saw no harm in a person breaking a voluntary fast, and he would strike examples for that: a man who circumambulated seven times, then stopped and did not complete it, he has what he counted; or one who prayed one rak'ah and did not pray another with it, he has what he counted; or one who sets out with wealth to give in charity, and gives some of it in charity and withholds some of it."
"7554","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, that Ibn 'Abbas said: Fasting is like charity: you intended to fast, then you changed your mind; and you intended to give charity, then you changed your mind."
"7555","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: Ibn 'Abbas saw no harm in breaking a voluntary fast."
"7556","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Whoever wakes up fasting voluntarily, if he wishes he continues his fast, and if he wishes he breaks it, and there is no making up upon him."
"7557","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, that he saw no harm in breaking a voluntary fast."
"7558","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said to his companions one day: ""What do you think is upon me? For I woke up this morning fasting, then I saw a slave-girl of mine and had intercourse with her."" So 'Ali said: ""You fasted voluntarily and you came to what is lawful; I see nothing upon you."""
"7559","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Sa'id ibn Abi al-Hasan, who said: I visited Ibn 'Abbas at the beginning of the day and found him fasting, then I visited him at the end of the day and found him not fasting. I said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I saw a slave-girl of mine and she pleased me, so I had intercourse with her. And let me tell you something more: she had committed immorality, so we gave her in marriage."""
"7560","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, and from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Umm al-Darda', and Qatadah said it: that Abu al-Darda', when he woke up, would ask his family for breakfast; if there was none, he would say: ""We are fasting."" From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm al-Darda', from Abu al-Darda', the same, except that he said: they said: ""unless fasting had been resolved upon (from the night)."""
"7561","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata informed me, from Umm al-Darda', from Abu al-Darda', that he used to come to his family until midday and ask them, saying: ""Is there any breakfast?"" Sometimes they found it and sometimes they did not, and he would say: ""Nothing but this day,"" and he would fast it, though he had woken up not fasting. And 'Ata claimed that he would do that: he would wake up not fasting until mid-morning and after it, then he would pass by, and perhaps he found breakfast or did not find it."
"7562","From 'Uthman, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Anas: that Abu Talhah used to come to his family and say: ""Is there any breakfast?"" If they said no, he would fast that day of his. Qatadah said: And Mu'adh ibn Jabal used to do that."
"7563","From Ma'mar, who said: I heard Qatadah say, from Ibn 'Abbas: The fasting person has the choice as long as breakfast has not been served."
"7564","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, I think from al-Harith, that 'Ali said: He has the choice until midday, as long as he has not tasted food or had resolved upon it (the fast) from the night."
"7565","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Talhah, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, who said: Hudhayfah said: Whoever decides to fast after the sun has passed its zenith, let him fast."
"7566","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mihran informed me that Abu Hurayrah and Abu Talhah would wake up not fasting, and would say: ""Is there any food?"" Whether they found it or not, they would complete that day (fasting)."
"7567","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, that a man came to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib and said: ""I woke up not intending to fast."" He said: ""You have the choice until midday; but if midday passes, it is not for you to break your fast."""
"7568","Ma'mar reported to us, from 'Ata al-Khurasani, who said: I used to fast one day and break my fast one day. I was on a journey, and it was my day of breaking fast. We traveled and did not alight until after midday, or at the time of prayer. He said: I said: ""I shall surely fast this day,"" so I fasted. Then I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyab, and he said: ""You did right."""
"7569","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, who said: I heard a man say: Ibn Mas'ud said: You have the choice until midday."
"7570","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: that Ibn 'Umar, when he resolved to fast, would not break his fast; and when he resolved to break his fast, he would not fast. Ma'mar said: And Ayyub informed me of it from Ibn 'Umar."
"7571","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Hafsah, who said: He said: ""There is no fast for one who did not resolve to fast from the night."" From Ibn Jurayj, and 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from Hafsah, the same."
"7572","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: When a man resolves to fast from the night, then wakes up fasting, he has the reward of the night and the reward of the day; but if he breaks his fast, making up is upon him."
"7573","From Ibrahim ibn 'Umar, from 'Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from al-Hasan and Ibrahim, who both said: If one resolves to fast from the night, then breaks his fast, making up is upon him. He said: And Ibrahim said: ""He should not break his fast except for an excuse."""
"7574","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: 'A'ishah and Hafsah woke up fasting, then some food was gifted to them and it pleased them, so they broke their fast. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them, Hafsah, who was her father's daughter, hastened to him and asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered them to fast a day in its place."
"7575","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ibn Shihab: ""Did 'Urwah narrate to you from 'A'ishah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever breaks a voluntary fast, let him make it up'?"" He said: ""I did not hear anything of that from 'Urwah; but someone narrated to me during the caliphate of Sulayman, from one of those who used to question 'A'ishah..."" then he mentioned the same as the hadith of Ma'mar from al-Zuhri."
"7576","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'A'ishah bint Talhah, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me one day and said: ""Do you have any food?"" She said: I said: ""No."" He said: ""Then I am fasting today."" She said: Then he came another time, and I said: ""Some habshish or hays has been gifted to us"" - 'Abd al-Razzaq doubted (which word it was) - and he said: ""Then I break my fast today, though I had resolved to fast."""
"7577","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from 'A'ishah bint Talhah, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me one day, and I brought him hays, and he ate some of it, and said: ""I had intended to fast today, but I shall fast a day in its place."""
"7578","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Qabus, from Abu Zabyan, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab entered the mosque and prayed one rak'ah, then left. He was asked about it, and he said: ""It is only voluntary; whoever wishes may add, and whoever wishes may do less. I disliked adopting it as a (fixed) way."""
"7579","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked 'Ata: ""Was it said: let a man break his fast outside the month of Ramadan for the sake of his guest?"" He said: ""Yes."""
"7580","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, who said: If the reciter (leading the night prayer) finds you praying the last 'Isha in Ramadan, having said takbir before him, then make your prayer the 'Isha: pray it with his prayer if he completes it (in full), otherwise differ from him and do not pray with his prayer. I said: ""He said takbir before me and I want to pray 'Isha?"" He said: ""Say takbir and make it 'Isha if he completes it; otherwise make it a supererogatory prayer, then pray 'Isha afterwards."""
"7581","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: When a man comes during the standing (night prayer) of Ramadan and has not prayed the prescribed prayer, he prays with them and counts it with them as the prescribed prayer."
"7582","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: He prays alone."
"7583","He said: His father Hammam informed him, who said: I heard Wahb praying alone, and I asked him about people who enter the mosque in the month of Ramadan, having already prayed the last 'Isha, while they (others) are standing in voluntary prayer: may they pray behind the imam in the mosque with one of them leading them? He said: ""No; they pray individually."""
"7584","From al-Thawri, from one who heard Ibrahim say: When you are in a prayer, do not join another with it. He says: ""When you are in a prescribed prayer, do not combine it with (another) prescribed (prayer)."" From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ibn 'Awn, from Ibn Sirin, the same."
"7585","From a man, who said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah informed me, who said: I came to the people while they were in the standing (night prayer), and I had not prayed 'Isha. So I prayed 'Isha for myself alone while they were praying. I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyab, and he said: ""You did right."" He said: Then he said to me: ""And what kept you from the prayer?"" So I excused myself to him, and he said: ""I have not seen the people for forty years"" - he means: I have not seen them departing - ""it never escaped me."""
"7586","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Abu al-Darda' used to keep alive the night of Friday (in worship) and fast its day. Salman came to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had established brotherhood between them, and stayed the night with him. Abu al-Darda' wanted to stay up his night, so Salman got up to him and did not let him until he slept and broke his fast (i.e. did not fast). He said: Abu al-Darda' came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Uwaymir! Salman knows better than you. Do not single out the night of Friday for prayer, nor its day for fasting."""
"7587","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited one of his wives on Friday while she was fasting, and said: ""Did you fast yesterday?"" She said: ""No."" He said: ""Do you intend to fast tomorrow?"" She said: ""No."" So he ordered her to break her fast."
"7588","From Abu Ma'shar, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting on Friday unless one fasts before it or after it."
"7589","From Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from a man, I think Abu al-Awbar, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: By the Lord of this Ka'bah, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding fasting on Friday unless joined with (other) fasting."
"7590","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Yahya ibn Ja'dah informed him, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Abd al-Qari, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: By the Lord of this House, I did not forbid fasting on Friday, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it. Then 'Amr says: ""(That is) when it is singled out."""
"7591","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah informed me that Muhammad ibn 'Abbad ibn Ja'far informed him that he asked Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah while he was circumambulating the House: ""Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding fasting on Friday?"" He said: ""Yes, by the Lord of this House."" From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, that he heard Muhammad ibn 'Abbad ibn Ja'far narrate this hadith."
"7592","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting on Friday except as part of one's usual fasting."
"7593","From Isra'il, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Qays ibn al-Sakan, who said: We set out as pilgrims and alighted at Abu Dharr's. He prepared food for us on a Friday, and among us was a fasting man. Then Abu Dharr said: ""I adjure you to eat, unless you have begun (fasting) the (whole) month,"" and he adjured him again once or twice. He said: ""Friday is a day of festival; your being not fasting is better for you."""
"7594","From Abu Ishaq, from 'Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, who said: Do not deliberately fast Friday."
"7595","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Imran ibn Zabyan al-Hanafi, from Hakim ibn Sa'd al-Hanafi, who said: I heard 'Ali say: Whoever among you voluntarily fasts days of the months, let Thursday be part of his fasting, and let him not deliberately (fast) Friday, for it is a day of festival and of food and drink. Then two good days come together for him: the day of his fasting, and the day of his (Friday) worship with the Muslims."
"7596","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke his fast at 'Arafah; Umm al-Fadl prepared milk for him, and he drank it at 'Arafah."
"7597","From 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, and al-Thawri, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from 'Umayr, the freedman of Umm al-Fadl, who said: They doubted about the fasting of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at 'Arafah, so Umm al-Fadl said: ""I shall find out that for you."" She sent him a cup of milk, and he drank from it."
"7598","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that he saw Ibn 'Abbas not fasting at 'Arafah, eating pomegranate."
"7599","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, who said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas invited (people) to food on the day of 'Arafah, and 'Abd Allah said: ""Do not fast, for milk in a milking vessel was brought near to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of 'Arafah and he drank; so do not fast, for the people follow your example."""
"7600","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata informed me that he heard 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr say: 'Umar went about on the day of 'Arafah among the dwellings of the pilgrims until the heat drove him to the tent of some people, and he was given sawiq (a barley drink) to drink, and he drank."
"7601","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, from a freedman of Ibn 'Abbas whom he named, who said: I visited Ibn 'Umar while he was eating on the day of 'Arafah. He said: ""Come near."" I said: ""I am fasting."" He said: ""Come near."" I said: ""If you wish, I shall do so."" He said: ""And will you tell the people that I ordered you to break your fast?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: Then he fell silent toward me; he neither ordered me nor forbade me."
"7602","From al-Thawri, from 'Uthman ibn Hakim, from Nudbah, a freedwoman of Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Ibn 'Abbas said on the day of 'Arafah: ""Let no one accompany us who intends to fast, for it is a day of takbir and of eating and drinking."" 'Abd al-Razzaq said: ""And al-Thawri forbade me to fast the day of Tarwiyah and the day of 'Arafah."""
"7603","From al-Thawri, from 'Urwah, and from 'Ata, who said: Whoever breaks his fast on the day of 'Arafah in order to gain strength thereby for supplication, he shall have the like of the reward of one fasting."
"7604","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I asked 'Ata: ""Do you fast the day of 'Arafah?"" He said: ""I fast it in winter, and I do not fast it in summer."""
"7605","From 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi': that Ibn 'Umar used to dislike fasting the day of 'Arafah."
"7606","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: There is no harm in fasting the day of 'Arafah."
"7607","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he would not fast the day of 'Arafah when he was traveling at 'Arafah, but when he was resident among his family he would fast it."
"7608","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from 'Abd Allah ibn Ma'bad, from Abu Qatadah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fasting the day of 'Arafah, and he said: ""It expiates the year before it."""
"7609","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Harmalah ibn Iyas al-Shaybani, from Abu Qatadah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fasting the day of 'Arafah, and he said: ""Expiation of two years: a past year and a coming year."""
"7610","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abu Qatadah, who said regarding fasting the day of 'Arafah: It expiates two years."
"7611","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from a man, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he did not fast the day of 'Arafah; and I performed Hajj with Abu Bakr and he did not fast it; and I performed Hajj with 'Umar and he did not fast it; and I performed Hajj with 'Uthman and he did not fast it. And I do not fast it, nor do I command it nor forbid it."
"7612","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father: that a man came to Hasan and Husayn on the day of 'Arafah and found one of them fasting and the other not fasting. He said: ""I came to ask you about a matter in which you differ."" They said: ""We do not differ: whoever fasts, it is good; and whoever does not fast, there is no harm."""
"7613","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, from 'Abd Allah ibn Ma'bad, from Abu Qatadah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fasting 'Ashura, and he said: ""Expiation of the year."""
"7614","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Harmalah ibn Iyas al-Shaybani, from Abu Qatadah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fasting the day of 'Ashura? He said: ""Expiation of the year."""
"7615","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata informed me, from Abu al-Khalil, from Qatadah, that he said regarding fasting the day of 'Ashura: It expiates the year."
"7616","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came to Madinah, said to a man of Aslam: ""Go to your people and order them: let them fast this day,"" for the day of 'Ashura. He said: ""What if I find some of them have already eaten breakfast?"" He said: ""Order them to complete it."""
"7617","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said, and Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that he heard Mu'awiyah preaching in Madinah, saying: ""O people of Madinah! Where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: 'This is the day of 'Ashura, and fasting it has not been made obligatory upon us; whoever of you wishes to fast, let him fast, for I am fasting.' So the people fasted."""
"7618","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ma'bad al-Qurashi, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at Qudayd when a man came to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""Have you eaten anything today,"" for the day of 'Ashura? He said: ""No, except that I drank water."" He said: ""Then do not eat after this until sunset, and order those behind you to fast this day."""
"7619","Ma'mar reported to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, who said: I never saw anyone more commanding of fasting the day of 'Ashura than 'Ali and Abu Musa."
"7620","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, that he heard Ibn 'Abbas say: I do not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would seek out the fast of a day, seeking its merit over others, except this day, the day of 'Ashura, or the month of Ramadan."
"7621","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith informed me, from his father, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab sent to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith on the night of 'Ashura: ""Eat suhur, and wake up fasting."" So 'Abd al-Rahman woke up fasting."
"7622","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: 'Ata informed me that he heard Ibn 'Abbas say regarding the day of 'Ashura: ""Differ from the Jews, and fast the ninth and the tenth."""
"7623","From Isma'il ibn 'Abd Allah, who said: Yunus ibn 'Ubayd informed me, from al-Hakam al-A'raj, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: When you wake up after the twenty-ninth, then wake up fasting, that is the day of 'Ashura."
"7624","Yunus said: And the nephew of al-Hakam informed me from him that he said: That is the day which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered to be fasted."
"7625","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Mas'ud ibn Fulan, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The day of 'Ashura is the tenth."
"7626","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abdah, from 'A'ishah, who said: We used to be ordered to fast the day of 'Ashura; then when the fasting of the month of Ramadan was revealed, whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished left it."
"7627","Ma'mar reported to us, and Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah and found the Jews fasting the day of 'Ashura. He said: ""What is this?"" They said: ""This is a great day in which Allah saved Musa and drowned the family of Pharaoh."" He said: So he fasted it in thanks. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I have more right to Musa and more right to his fasting than you."" So he fasted it and ordered its fasting."
"7628","Ma'mar reported to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came to Madinah, fasted the day of 'Ashura and ordered its fasting. Then when Ramadan was made obligatory, whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished left it."
"7629","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn 'Urwah informed me, from his father, from 'A'ishah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Quraysh in the pre-Islamic days used to fast it; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered, and he fasted it when he came to Madinah, and ordered its fasting before Ramadan was made obligatory. Then when Ramadan was made obligatory, it was the obligatory fast. 'A'ishah said: ""Whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished left it."""
"7630","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, who said: Al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah narrated to me, from Abu 'Ammar, who said: We asked Qays ibn Sa'd about Zakat al-Fitr? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us with it before Zakat was revealed; then when Zakat was revealed, he neither ordered us nor forbade us."
"7631","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', who said: Ibn 'Umar would not fast the day of 'Ashura when traveling, but when resident he would fast it."
"7632","From 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah and found the Jews fasting the day of 'Ashura, and they said: ""This is a great day which the Jews venerate."" So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""We have more right to venerate it."" So he fasted it and ordered its fasting. Then when fasting (of Ramadan) was revealed, whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished left it."
"7633","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah, who said: Nuh boarded the Ark in Rajab with ten days remaining, and disembarked from the Ark on the day of 'Ashura."
"7634","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Sayfi informed me that 'Amr ibn Abi Yusuf, of Banu Nawfal, informed him that he heard Mu'awiyah on the pulpit say: ""The day of 'Ashura is a day of festival; whoever fasts it, it used to be fasted; and whoever leaves it, there is no harm."""
"7635","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard 'Ata claim that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered fasting the day of 'Ashura. They said: ""What about one who ate?"" He said: ""Whoever ate or did not eat (let him fast/complate it)."""
"7636","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from 'Ikrimah, who said: It is the day on which Allah accepted Adam's repentance, the day of 'Ashura."
"7637","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not take a month as a festival, nor take a day as a festival."""
"7638","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, who said: Ibn 'Abbas used to forbid fasting all of Rajab, lest it be taken as a festival."
"7639","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, who said: Ibn 'Abbas used to forbid fasting the whole month, saying: ""Let him fast it except (some) days."" And he used to forbid singling out the day each time it comes, and fasting the well-known (fixed) days; and he used to say: ""Do not fast a known (fixed) fast."""
"7640","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: that Ibn 'Umar used to fast the sacred months."
"7641","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi': that Ibn 'Umar would hardly ever break his fast in the sacred months, nor outside them."
"7642","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: Some people who fast Rajab were mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Where are they with regard to Sha'ban?"" Zayd said: And the most fasting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Ramadan was Sha'ban."
"7643","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, who said: Ibn Abi Labid narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, who said: I asked 'A'ishah about the fasting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: He would fast until we said: he has fasted; and break his fast until we said: he has broken his fast. And I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fast more in a month than his fasting in Sha'ban, except what was Ramadan. He would fast all of Sha'ban except a little. He said: And I asked her about his prayer? She said: The prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan and outside it was thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of Fajr."
"7644","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from 'Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, who said: I asked 'A'ishah about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed standing, he would bow standing; and when he prayed sitting, he would bow sitting. He said: And I asked her about his fasting? She said: When he fasted, he would fast until we said: he fasted, he fasted, he fasted; and when he broke his fast, he would break it until we said: he broke his fast, he broke his fast, he broke his fast. And I never knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to have fasted a complete month since he came to Madinah."
"7645","From Malik ibn Anas, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, freedman of 'Umar ibn 'Ubayd Allah, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast until we said: he does not break his fast; and break his fast until we said: he does not fast. And I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complete a month at all except Ramadan, and I never saw him in any month fasting more than in Sha'ban."
"7646","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and said: ""Have I not been told that you say - or: are you the one who says: I shall surely stand the night and fast the day?"" He said: I think he said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said: ""So stand and sleep, and break your fast and fast: fast three days of every month, for that is like the fasting of a lifetime."" He said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah! I can do better than that."" He said: ""Then fast one day and break your fast two days,"" until I said: ""I can do better than that."" He said: ""Then fast one day and break your fast one day; that is the most just fast, and it is the fast of Dawud."" He said: I said: ""I can do better than that."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is nothing better than that."""
"7647","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard 'Ata, that Abu al-'Abbas the poet informed him that he heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr say: It reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that I fast continuously without breaking, and pray the night. Either he sent for me or I met him, and he said: ""Have I not been told that you fast and do not break your fast, and you pray? Do not do that, for your eye has a share, and your self has a share. So fast and break your fast, and fast one day out of ten days, and you have the reward of nine."" He said: ""I find myself stronger than that, O Prophet of Allah!"" He said: ""Then fast the fast of Dawud."" He said: ""And how did he fast, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""He would fast one day and break his fast one day, and he would not flee when he met (the enemy)."" 'Ata said: I do not know how he mentioned the fasting of a lifetime? Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""He who fasts a lifetime has not fasted; he who fasts a lifetime has not fasted."""
"7648","From Ibn Jurayj and Ibn 'Uyaynah, who both said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed us that 'Amr ibn Aws informed him, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The most beloved fasting to Allah is the fasting of Dawud; he would fast half a lifetime. And the most beloved prayer to Allah is the prayer of Dawud; he would sleep half the night, then stand a third of it, then sleep a sixth of it. And he would not flee when he met (the enemy)."""
"7649","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from 'Abd Allah ibn Ma'bad, from Abu Qatadah, who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him: ""How is your fasting?"" He turned away from him - and when he was asked about something he disliked, it was known on his face - and he fell silent until the anger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went away. Then 'Umar said to him: ""What do you say, O Messenger of Allah, about the fasting of a lifetime?"" He said: ""He neither fasted nor broke his fast"" - or he said: ""He did not fast and did not break his fast."" He said: ""What do you say about fasting two days and breaking one day?"" He said: ""And who can bear that?"" He said: ""Fasting one day and breaking two days?"" He said: ""I wish I could bear that."" He said: ""Fasting one day and breaking one day?"" He said: ""That is the fasting of Dawud."" He said: ""What do you say about fasting three days of every month?"" He said: ""That is the fasting of a lifetime."" He said: ""Fasting Monday?"" He said: ""That is a day on which I was born, and a day on which revelation was sent down upon me."" He said: ""Fasting 'Ashura?"" He said: ""Expiation of a year."" He said: ""Fasting the day of 'Arafah?"" He said: ""Expiation of a year and what is before it."""
"7650","From al-Thawri, from Abu Tamimah al-Hujaymi, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: Whoever fasts a lifetime, Allah narrows Hell upon him like this - and he closed ten (fingers, i.e. made a circle?)."
"7651","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu 'Ammar, from 'Amr ibn Shurahbil, from a man of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah! What about a man who fasts the whole lifetime?"" He said: ""I wish he never tasted anything for a lifetime."" He said: ""Two-thirds of it?"" He said: ""Too much."" He said: ""Half of it?"" He said: ""Too much."" He said: ""A third of it?"" He said: ""It did not come down (i.e. no answer was revealed?)."" ""Shall I not tell you what removes the rancor of the chest: fasting three days of every month."""
"7652","Al-Thawri reported to us, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Salil, from a man whom he named, from his father, from his uncle, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""Who are you?"" He said: ""I am the one who came to you last year."" He said: ""You seem leaner than I found you"" - or ""thinner in body than I see."" He said: ""I have not tasted (food) since I parted from you except at night."" He said: ""Who ordered you to torment yourself?"" three times. He said: ""I am strong enough."" He said: ""Then fast the month of patience (Ramadan), and one day of every month."" He said: ""I am stronger."" He said: ""Then fast the fast of the month, and three days of every month."" He said: ""I am stronger."" He said: ""Then fast of the sacred (months), and break your fast."""
"7653","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, who said: My father fasted forty years or thirty years, not breaking his fast except on the day of Fitr or the day of sacrifice; and he died while fasting."
"7654","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: Abu Talhah fasted the least in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he was a Badri - because of fighting. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, I never saw him breaking his fast except on the day of Adha or the day of Fitr."
"7655","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Harun ibn Sa'd, from Abu 'Amr al-Shaybani, who said: We were with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab when some food of his was brought, and a man of the people withdrew. He said: ""What is wrong with him?"" They said: ""He is fasting."" He said: ""And what is his fasting?"" He said: ""A lifetime (continuously)."" He said: He began striking his head with a spear-shaft he had, saying: ""Eat, O lifetime! Eat, O lifetime!"""
"7656","Ma'mar reported to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, who said: The fasting of the month of patience (Ramadan), and the fasting of three days of every month, remove the disturbances of the chest. Abu Ishaq said: And Mujahid said: ""They remove the rancor of the chest."" It was said: ""And what is the rancor of the chest?"" He said: ""Its deceit."""
"7657","Ma'mar reported to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Abu Dharr, who said: I think he raised it (to the Prophet): that he was commanded to fast the white (bright) days: the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth."
"7658","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, from Musa ibn Talhah, from a man of Banu Tamim called Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, from 'Umar, that he said: Who of those present was with us on the day of al-Qahah? When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a hare, Abu Dharr said: ""I was. An Arab Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a hare and said: 'I saw it bleeding.' He said: 'Eat of it' - and he mentioned that he himself did not eat - and the Bedouin said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'And what is your fasting?' He mentioned something, and he said: 'Where are you with regard to the bright white days: the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth?'"""
"7659","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Three things which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me: that I sleep upon witr, and fasting three days of every month, and the two rak'ahs of Duha. Qatadah said: Then al-Hasan afterwards dropped in this hadith the two rak'ahs of Duha, and put in its place ""the washing of Friday."""
"7660","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: Three things I shall not leave until I meet Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that I spend every night upon witr, and the Duha prayer, and that I fast three days of every month."
"7661","Ma'mar reported to us, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu al-'Ala ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, who said: A Bedouin came to us while we were at al-Mirbad, and said: ""Is there among you a reader who can read this sheet?"" We said: ""All of us."" He said: ""Then read it for me."" He said: ""This is a letter which Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), wrote for me for Banu Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh, a clan of 'Ukl: 'If you bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and establish prayer, and give zakat, and take out the fifth of the spoils, and the share of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his choice portion, then you are safe with the security of Allah.'"" He said: We said: ""Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) write this letter for you?"" He said: ""Yes. Do you think I lie about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"" And he became angry, and struck his hand upon the letter, and took it."
"7662","He said: So we followed him, and said: ""Narrate to us, O Abu 'Abd Allah, something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."" He said: I heard him say: ""Part of what removes much of the rancor of the chest is fasting the month of patience (Ramadan), and fasting three days of every month."" 'Abd al-Razzaq said: ""The choice portion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a share called al-safi; he would take it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would draw (a share) with a سهم of the Muslims."""
"7663","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu al-'Ala ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Nu'aym ibn Qu'nab, who said: I set out to al-Rabadhah seeking Abu Dharr, but I did not find him. I greeted his wife and said: ""Where is Abu Dharr?"" She said: ""He went to toil."" He said: I sat down, and Abu Dharr came leading two camels, one tied to the tail of the other, with a water-skin on the neck of each. He made the two camels kneel and carried the two water-skins. I greeted him, and he spoke to his wife about something, and she seemed to answer him back, and he repeated and she repeated. Then he said: ""You do not go beyond what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The woman is like the rib: if you straighten it, it breaks; and in it is sufficiency and crookedness.' Then he brought a tray with something like a sand-grouse in it, and said: ""Eat, for I am fasting."" Then he stood to pray, then returned and ate with him. Nu'aym said: ""To Allah we belong, O Abu Dharr! Whoever of the people deceived me - as for you, I never thought you would deceive me."" He said: ""I did not deceive you. Rather I said: I am fasting, then I ate. And now I say to you: I am fasting. I fasted three days of this month, so its fasting became due for me, and its breaking became lawful for me."""
"7664","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu 'Ubayd, freedman of 'Abd al-Rahman, that he heard 'Umar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid fasting these two days - meaning Fitr and Adha. He said: As for one of them, it is the day of your breaking from your fasting; and as for the other, it is a day in which you eat of your sacrifices."
"7665","From Ma'mar, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ata ibn Mina, that he heard him narrate from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: Two days of fasting are forbidden, and two sales, and two garments. As for the two days: the day of Fitr and the day of Adha. As for the two sales: mulamasah and munabadhah. Mulamasah is that each of them touches the garment of his fellow without examining; and munabadhah is that each of them throws his garment to the other, neither of them looking at the garment of his fellow. As for the two garments: that a man sits in one garment with his private part exposed; and as for the other garment: that one throws the inside of his waist-wrapper and its outside upon one of his shoulders, baring his side. 'Amr said: They held that if one sits in a garment covering his private part, there is no harm."
"7666","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: I said to 'Amr: ""What if one joins the two ends of the garment upon his right side?"" He said: ""I never saw them but disliking that, were it done."""
"7667","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade two garments and two sales. As for the two garments: ishtimal al-samma' - wrapping in one garment, placing the two ends of the garment upon his left shoulder; and the other: that one sits in one garment with nothing else upon him, exposing his private part to the sky. As for the two sales: munabadhah and mulamasah. Munabadhah is that he says: ""When I throw this garment, (the sale) is concluded."" And mulamasah is that he touches it with his hand, without unfolding it or turning it over; when he touches it, the sale is concluded."
"7668","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Two sales and two garments were forbidden, and prayer at two times, and two meals, and fasting two days. As for the two sales and the two garments, it is as al-Zuhri said. As for prayer at two times: after 'Asr and after dawn. As for fasting two days: the day of Fitr and the day of Adha. As for the two meals: joining two dates together (eating them as one), and the other: that one eats while standing."
"7669","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from 'Umar ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mulamasah and munabadhah - then he mentioned the same as the hadith of Ma'mar from al-Zuhri."
"7670","From al-Thawri, from Abu 'Abbad, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting six days: the day before Ramadan, the day of Adha, the day of Fitr, and the three days of Tashriq."
"7671","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Let no woman fast voluntarily while her husband is present except with his permission; and let her not admit anyone to his house while he is present except with his permission. Whatever she spends of his earnings without his order, half its reward is for him."""
"7672","Ma'mar reported to us, from Qatadah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a woman to fast except with her husband's permission, voluntarily."
"7673","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Sulayman ibn Musa said to 'Ata: It was said: ""Let a woman break her fast for her husband, and a man for his guest?"" He said: ""Yes; and if she is praying, let her turn to him."""
"7674","A man reported to us, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'amah, who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: It is not lawful for a woman to fast voluntarily except with her husband's permission."
"7675","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a woman to fast a day outside Ramadan except with her husband's permission."
"7676","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every deed of the son of Adam is for him, except fasting; for fasting is for Me, and I reward for it. And the breath of the fasting person's mouth is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk."""
"7677","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, the breath of the fasting person's mouth is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk. He leaves his desire, and his food and his drink, for My sake; so fasting is for Me, and I reward for it."""
"7678","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every good deed the son of Adam does is multiplied ten to seven hundred times, except fasting; it is for Me, and I reward for it. He leaves his desire for My sake, and leaves his food for My sake."""
"7679","Two joys for the fasting person: a joy at his breaking his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord."
"7680","And the breath of his mouth is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk; and fasting is for Me, and I reward for it."
"7681","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Fasting is a man's shield, like the shield of one of you in battle; and the chief of days is Friday, and the chief of months is the month of Ramadan. And judge people by their companions, for a man befriends only one whose path or condition pleases him."
"7682","From Hisham, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Abu al-'Aliyah, who said: The fasting person is in worship as long as he does not backbite anyone, even if sleeping on his bed. And Hafsah used to say: ""How excellent a worship, while I am sleeping on my bed."""
"7683","Hisham said: And Hafsah said: Fasting is a shield, as long as its owner does not tear it; and its tearing is backbiting."
"7684","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ka'b said: The fasting person is in worship as long as he does not backbite."
"7685","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Wasil, from Laqit, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: The people campaigned by land and sea, and I was among those who campaigned by sea. While we were sailing in the sea, we heard a voice saying: ""O people of the ship! Stop, I shall inform you."" We looked right and left, and saw nothing but the expanse of the sea. Then it called a second time, until it called seven times, saying the same. Abu Musa said: When it was the seventh time, I stood up and said: ""What will you inform us?"" He said: ""I inform you of a decree Allah has decreed upon Himself: that whoever makes himself thirsty for Allah on a hot day, He will quench him on the Day of Resurrection."" Abu Burdah said: Abu Musa would hardly let a hot day pass but he would fast it, and he would writhe in it from thirst."
"7686","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The fasting person has two joys: a joy at his breaking the fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord, and the changed smell of the fasting person's mouth is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk."
"7687","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Abi Ya'qub, from Abu Umama, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition, and I went out with them, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to grant me martyrdom. He said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them booty. He said: So we returned safe and with booty. He said: Then he sent an army, and I went out with them, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to grant me martyrdom, and he said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them booty. Then the third time the same. And I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came to you asking you three times to supplicate for martyrdom for me, and you said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them booty, so we returned safe and with booty, O Messenger of Allah, so command me to an act. He said: Hold to fasting, for it has no equal and no counterpart. Abu Umama said: So Allah granted much good from that. And Ma'mar mentioned it from Abu Umama."
"7688","From Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umm Ayman migrated to Allah and to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while she was fasting, with no provision, no mount, and no waterskin, in the severe heat of Tihama, and she almost died of hunger and thirst, until when it was the time when the fasting person breaks fast, she heard a rustle above her head, so she raised her head, and behold a bucket hung by a white rope. She said: So I took it with my hands and drank from it until I was quenched, and I never thirsted after that. He said: So she used to fast and circumambulate in order to thirst in her fast, but she could not become thirsty until she died."
"7689","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Three are from the character of prophethood, and they are beneficial, or he said: wholesome for phlegm: fasting, the toothstick, and prayer at the end of the night, meaning recitation of the Qur'an."
"7690","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubayda, from his mother, she said: I never saw Abdullah ibn Mas'ud fasting at all except two days, apart from Ramadan. She said: I do not know what was the matter of those two days."
"7691","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah used to fast little. So we said to him: You fast little. He said: When I fast I become weak for prayer, and prayer is dearer to me than fasting."
"7692","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, he said: We were with Abdullah, and drink was brought, and he said: Hand it to the people. They said: We are fasting. He said: But I am not fasting, and he drank. Then he recited: They fear a Day in which hearts and eyes will turn about."
"7693","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever gives breakfast to a fasting person, feeding him and giving him drink, will have the like of his reward, without anything being diminished from his reward."
"7694","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'ama, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra saying: Whoever gives breakfast to a fasting person, feeding him and giving him drink, will have the like of his reward."
"7695","From Ma'mar, from Thabit, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate oil at Sa'd ibn Ubada's, then said: The fasting people broke fast with you, the righteous ate your food, and the angels descended upon you."
"7696","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Hurayra, a woman invited him to break fast with her, and he did so, and said: I tell you that there is no man who breaks fast with a household except they will have the like of his reward. She said: I wish you would choose times, or the like of that, to break fast with me. He said: I want to reserve it for my household."
"7697","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: When food is eaten in the presence of the fasting person, the angels send blessings upon him."
"7698","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Dharr al-Hamdani, from Yazid ibn Halil al-Nakha'i, he said: When food is eaten in the presence of the fasting person, his joints glorify Allah."
"7699","Al-Thawri said, and Isma'il ibn Salim al-Asadi informed me, from Mujahid, he said: When food is eaten in the presence of the fasting person, the angels glorify Allah."
"7700","From Sufyan, from Shu'ba, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from a woman called Layla, from Umm Umara, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, and we brought food near to him, and some of those with him were fasting. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you eat with a fasting person, the angels send blessings upon him."
"7701","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: It is recommended for the fasting person to oil himself until the dust of fasting leaves him."
"7702","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, he said: Jesus son of Mary used to say: When it is the fast day of one of you, let him oil his beard and wipe his lips, until he goes out to the people and they say: He is not fasting. And when one of you prays, let him draw the curtain of his door, for Allah distributes praise as He distributes provision. And when one of you gives, let him give with his right hand and conceal it from his left."
"7703","Ma'mar informed us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The gates of heaven are opened every Monday and Thursday, and Allah forgives every servant who does not associate with Allah, except the two quarrelers. The angels say: Leave them until they reconcile."
"7704","From Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabra, he said: Muslim ibn Abi Maryam informed me, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast the day of Monday and Thursday, for they are two days in which deeds are raised, and Allah forgives every servant who does not associate with Him, except one with rancour. Allah says: Leave him until he repents."
"7705","From Abu Shayba, he said: al-Hakam ibn Utayba informed me that Mujahid used to fast Monday and Thursday, and say: They are two days in which deeds are raised, so I love that my deed be raised while I am fasting."
"7706","From a man of the people of Medina, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz used to fast the day of Monday and Thursday. He said: And a shaykh of Ghifar informed me that he heard Sa'id al-Maqburi narrating, from Abu Hurayra, from Usama ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used not to leave fasting Monday and Thursday, and said: They are two days in which deeds are presented, so I love that a righteous deed be presented for me therein."
"7707","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Sa'd ibn Sa'id ibn Qays, brother of Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal, fasting of the year is written for him, saying: For every day ten days. And by it we take. From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sabra, from Sa'd ibn Sa'id, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"7708","From Zam'a, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six days of Shawwal, fasting of a year is written for him."
"7709","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sa'd, brother of Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to me, from Umar ibn Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj of Banu al-Khazraj, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and follows it with six days of Shawwal, that is fasting of the age. He said: I said: For every day ten? He said: Yes."
"7710","And I asked Ma'mar about fasting the six that are after the day of Fitr, and they said to him: Are they fasted the day after Fitr? So he said: Allah forbid. They are days of festival, eating and drinking. But three days are fasted before the white days, or the three white days, or after them. And the white days are the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth. And we asked Abd al-Razzaq about one who fasts the second day? And he disliked that and refused it strongly."
"7711","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Mak-hul narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Murra, that Allah looks upon the servants on the night of the middle of Sha'ban and forgives the people of the earth except a polytheist man or a quarrelsome one. From al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, he said: Qays ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Mak-hul, from Kathir ibn Murra, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of the hadith of Muhammad ibn Rashid."
"7712","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mis'ar, from a man, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: The lifespans are copied out in the middle of Sha'ban, such that a man goes out travelling though he has been copied from the living to the dead, and he marries though he has been copied from the living to the dead."
"7713","From Hushaym, from Uthman ibn Hakim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: A man walks in the markets while his name is among the dead."
"7714","And one who heard al-Baylamani informed me, he narrating from his father, from Ibn Umar, he said: Five nights in which supplication is not rejected: the night of Friday, the first night of Rajab, the night of the middle of Sha'ban, and the two nights of the two festivals."
"7715","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, he said: It was said to Ibn Abi Mulayka: Ziyad al-Minqari, and he was a storyteller, says the reward of the night of the middle of Sha'ban is like the reward of the Night of Decree. So Ibn Abi Mulayka said: If I heard him saying that with a stick in my hand, I would strike him with it."
"7716","From Ma'mar, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Ala ibn Abdullah ibn Shikhkhir, he said: A woman narrated to me that she heard Umar ibn al-Khattab while he was preaching, saying: O company of women, when you dye with henna, beware of engraved designs and edging, and let one of you dye her hands up to here, and he pointed to the place of the bracelet."
"7717","From Ma'mar and others, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about dye for women. He said: As for our women, they dye when they have prayed Isha, then they leave their hands free until morning, then they redo it until Zuhr prayer, so perfect the dye, and it does not prevent them from prayer. Abd al-Razzaq said: That is because I asked Ma'mar how do you dye your beard? So he narrated this to me."
"7718","From Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pledge allegiance to him. He said: Why do you not dye with henna? Do you have a husband? She said: Yes. He said: Then dye, for a woman dyes for two matters: if she has a husband, let her dye for her husband, and if she has no husband, let her dye for her betrothal. Then he said: Allah has cursed the masculinized among women and the effeminate among men."
"7719","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he disliked that a woman pray with no necklace on her neck."
"7720","Umar ibn Rashid informed us, from Ishaq ibn Abi Talha, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever perfumes himself for Allah will come on the Day of Resurrection with his scent sweeter than musk, and whoever perfumes himself for other than Allah will come on the Day of Resurrection with his scent fouler than a carcass."
"7721","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: They used not to recognize the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by the scent of perfume."
"7722","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, he said: Surayya bint Dhakwan informed me, she said: We used to bring Umar ghaliya and dharira with that musk, and he would begin and dye his beard with khaluq, perfume his beard with ghaliya, sprinkle himself with powder, and use incense."
"7723","Ma'mar informed us, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, he said: Ammar ibn Yasir came, and his family daubed him with yellow perfume. He said: Then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted him. He said: And upon you be peace, go and wash. He said: So I went and washed, then returned with traces of yellow still on me, and said: Peace be upon you. He said: Upon you be peace, go and wash. He said: So I went and washed, then returned with its trace still on me, until I did that several times. Then I went the third time and took a coarse cloth and rubbed my skin with it until I thought I had cleansed my skin. Then I came and said: Peace be upon you. He said: Upon you be peace, sit. Then he said: The angels do not attend the funeral of a disbeliever with good, nor one in major impurity until he bathes or performs his ablution for prayer, nor one daubed with yellow perfume."
"7724","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abdullah ibn Hafs, from Ya'la ibn Murra al-Thaqafi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me while I was smeared with khaluq. He said: Do you have a wife? I said: No. He said: Go and wash it, then do not repeat, three times. He said: So I washed it, then washed it, then I never repeated."
"7725","From Ibn Uyayna, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take pledges from people, and a man came with traces of khaluq on him, and he pledged him with the tips of his fingers. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best perfume of men is what its scent is apparent and its colour hidden, and the best perfume of women is what its colour is apparent and its scent hidden."
"7726","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, and from Layth, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perfume and women were made beloved to me, and the coolness of my eye was placed in prayer."
"7727","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he used to dislike that the mushaf be vowel-pointed or anything added in it."
"7728","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, that he used to dislike dotting and marking tenths in the mushaf. Sufyan said: I think it means the dots of Arabic grammar."
"7729","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Abu Husayn, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Masruq, that Ibn Mas'ud used to dislike marking tenths in the mushaf."
"7730","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: It was disliked that perfume and tenth-markings be put in the mushaf."
"7731","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Za'ra, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: Strip the Qur'an bare, saying: Do not clothe it with what is not from it."
"7732","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that Ali used to dislike that small mushafs be made."
"7733","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: It used to be said: Magnify the Qur'an, meaning the mushafs, and do not make them small."
"7734","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: O people, learn the Qur'an, for one of you does not know when it will be made to seem to him. He said: A man came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what do you say about a man who recites the Qur'an reversed? He said: That is one whose heart is reversed. He said: And a mushaf was brought that had been adorned and gilded. He said: Abdullah said: The best adornment of the mushaf is its recitation in truth."
"7735","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'ba, he said: Muhammad ibn Sayf Abu Raja informed me, he said: I asked al-Hasan about the mushaf, whether it is dotted in Arabic? He said: No harm in it. Has the letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab not reached you? He wrote: Learn understanding of religion, do dream interpretation well, and learn Arabic. He said: And I asked Ibn Sirin, and he said: I fear letters would be added. He said: And Mansur informed me, he said: I asked al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin about it, and they said: No harm in it."
"7736","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, he said: Hafs ibn Maysara informed me, from a man of the descendants of Hudhayfa, that Hudhayfa said: I shall strive tonight in supplication. He said: A softening of the heart seized him and he could do nothing. He said: Then he heard a speaker saying: Say: O Allah our Lord, to You is all praise, in Your hand is all good, to You the whole matter returns, its open and its secret. Worthy to be praised. You are over all things capable. O Allah, forgive me my past sins, protect me in the rest of my life, and grant me pure deeds You are pleased with from me."
"7737","From Ma'mar, he said: Ja'far ibn Abi Talib married Asma bint Umays al-Khath'amiyya, and he was killed leaving her, then Abu Bakr married her and died leaving her, then Ali ibn Abi Talib married her after Fatima."
"7738","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, and Hisham ibn Urwa from his father, they said: When a master marries off his slave, he has no right to separate between them."
"7739","From al-Zuhri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Mas'ud used to raise his hands in witr, then let them down after."
"7740","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, from Habiba bint Maysara ibn Abi Khaytham, from Umm Bani Kurz al-Ka'biyya, that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the aqiqa. He said: For a boy two matching sheep, and for a girl one sheep. She said: I said: What is matching? He said: Alike. And sheep are dearer to me than goats; its males are dearer to him than its females, as his opinion."
"7741","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me that Siba' ibn Thabit claims that Muhammad ibn Thabit ibn Siba' informed him that Umm Kurz informed him that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the aqiqa. He said: Yes, for a boy two, and for a girl one, and it does not harm you whether they are males or females."
"7742","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me, from some of his family, that he heard A'isha saying: Is it not for a boy two sheep and for a girl one sheep, and it does not harm you whether male or female, she attributes that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I heard him saying it."
"7743","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed us, he said: I and Ibn Mulayka entered upon Hafsa bint Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and she had borne al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr a boy, and I said: Why have you not sacrificed a camel for your son? She said: Allah forbid. My aunt A'isha used to say: For a boy two sheep and for a girl one sheep."
"7744","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: For a boy two sheep."
"7745","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With a boy is an aqiqa, so shed blood for him and remove harm from him. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"7746","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Qatada, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed aqiqa for himself after he was sent with prophethood."
"7747","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrating from his father from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the aqiqa? He said: I do not love disobedience, as if he disliked the name. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we ask you about one of us to whom a child is born. He said: Whoever of you loves to sacrifice for his child, let him do so: for a boy two matching sheep, and for a girl one sheep."
"7748","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed aqiqa for Hasan and Husayn with two rams."
"7749","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told a hadith raised to A'isha that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed aqiqa for Hasan with two sheep and for Husayn with two sheep, slaughtering them on the seventh day. He said: And he wiped them, and commanded that harm be removed from their heads. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Slaughter upon his name and say: In the name of Allah, O Allah, for You and to You, this is the aqiqa of so-and-so. He said: And the people of ignorance used to dye cotton with the blood of the aqiqa, and when they shaved the boy they placed it on his head, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to put khaluq perfume in place of blood, meaning by he wiped them: there was placed on their heads mashq clay like khaluq."
"7750","From Abdullah ibn Umar, and Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar, no one of his family would ask him for an aqiqa but he would give it to him. He said: And he used to say: For a boy one sheep and for a girl one sheep."
"7751","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Abu al-Nadr, from Makhul, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The newborn is pledged for his aqiqa. He said: And it reached me from Ibn Umar that he used to say it."
"7752","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: A boy is pledged for his aqiqa, he used to narrate it, and if a sacrifice is offered for him that suffices him for the aqiqa."
"7753","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: Whoever had no aqiqa performed for him, his sacrificial animal suffices him. Ibn Jurayj said: It is cooked with water and salt in pieces, or he said: limbs, and given to neighbours and friends, and nothing of it is given as charity."
"7754","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: Aqiqa is performed for a boy with a sheep, and no aqiqa for a girl; there is no aqiqa upon her."
"7755","From Ibn Uyayna, he said: I heard Ata saying: Aqiqa is performed for him on his seventh day, and if they miss it, I prefer they delay it to the next seventh. He said: And I saw people aiming to perform aqiqa for him on his seventh day. He said: The family of the aqiqa eat and gift it. I said to him: Is it sunna? He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that, they claim. I said: Do I give charity? He said: No, if you wish eat and gift. It was said: Two slaughtered? He said: No, except upright."
"7756","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Slaughtering is begun with before shaving."
"7757","Ibn Jurayj said: I also found a writing from Ata, he said: Shaving is begun with before slaughtering."
"7758","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: He is named, then aqiqa is performed on his seventh day, then he is shaved. And he used to say: His head is smeared with blood."
"7759","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, he said: Aqiqa is performed for him and he is named on his seventh day, and if no aqiqa is performed, the sacrificial animal suffices for him."
"7760","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ali saying: Fatima daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), no child was born to her but she commanded him to be shaved, then gave charity of the weight of his hair in silver. She said: And my father used to do that."
"7761","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ja'far, he said: Fatima, when she gave birth, would shave his hair, then give charity of its weight in silver."
"7762","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me that he heard al-Hasan ibn Muhammad saying: Let the boy be left until his seventh day, then shaved."
"7763","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach a boy of Banu Hashim when he became articulate, seven times: Praise be to Allah who has not taken a son and has no partner in dominion, to the end of the sura."
"7764","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to like, when a child first speaks clearly, to teach him: There is no god but Allah seven times, so it is the first thing he speaks."
"7765","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me from al-Hasan that he said: If he dies before his seventh, there is no aqiqa upon him."
"7766","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named Husayn on his seventh day, and that he derived the name Husayn from Hasan, and it was mentioned that between them was only the pregnancy."
"7767","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: al-Hasan ibn Ali was the most resembling of them to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7768","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrima, he said: When Fatima bore al-Hasan ibn Ali, she brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he named him Hasan. And when she bore Husayn, she brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is more beautiful than this, meaning Husayn, so he split a name for him from his name and named him Husayn."
"7769","Ma'mar informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: My mother made for me on the day I was circumcised nothing but asida with dates."
"7770","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Mubarak ibn Fadala, he said: I heard al-Hasan saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A boy was born to me tonight, and I named him with the name of my father Ibrahim. From al-Thawri, from one of his companions, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"7771","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when a child was born to him, would take him as he was in his swaddling cloth, call the adhan in his right ear, call the iqama in his left, and name him on the spot."
"7772","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) call the adhan of prayer in the ear of al-Hasan ibn Ali when Fatima bore him."
"7773","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek protection for Hasan and Husayn, saying: I seek protection for you both with the perfect words of Allah from every devil and vermin, and from every harmful eye. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek protection with them for your children, for Ibrahim, peace be upon him, used to seek protection with them for his two sons Isma'il and Ishaq. From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Minhal, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas the like of it."
"7774","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: The people of ignorance used to slaughter of the fara'a one out of every fifty. When Islam came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about that? He said: If you wish, do so, and he did not make it obligatory."
"7775","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, they said: Ibn Tawus informed us that his father informed him, he said: The people of ignorance used to offer the fara'. When Islam came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about that? He said: If you wish, offer the fara', and that you leave it until it grows and you mount it in the path of Allah is better than that you slaughter it and its flesh mixes with its hair. Ibn Jurayj said: A man said to him: What about cattle and sheep? He said: It was dearer to Abu Abd al-Rahman that they be fed until they grow, then the poor be fed with them."
"7776","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Tawus and Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the fara'. He said: Offer the fara' if you wish, and that you leave it until it grows and is mounted in the path of Allah, or you join kinship with it, is better than that you slaughter it and its flesh mixes with its hair."
"7777","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ibn Abi Ammar informed him, from Abu Hurayra, that he said about the fara'a: It is a right, but do not slaughter it while it is a sticky newborn clinging to your hand; rather give it time with milk, until when it is of the best wealth, slaughter it. Amr said: A man informed me that he heard it from Abu Hurayra."
"7778","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abi Ammar, he said: Abu Hurayra was asked about the fara'a? He said: It is a right, but not that you slaughter it a sticky newborn; rather give it time with milk, until when it is your most precious wealth, you slaughter it or mount it."
"7779","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the fara'a. He said: Offer the fara' if you wish."
"7780","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrating from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the fara'a. He said: The fara'a is a right, and that you leave it until it is a sturdy yearling camel or two-year-old, then mount it in the path of Allah, or give it to a widow, is better than that you slaughter it with its flesh sticking to its fur, overturn your vessel, and bereave its mother camel."
"7781","From Ma'mar and Ibn Uyayna, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man of Banu Damra, from his father or his uncle, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the fara'. He said: It is a right, and that you leave it until it is a sturdy yearling or two-year-old camel is better than that you overturn your vessel, bereave its mother camel, slaughter it, and its hair mixes, or he said: sticks, to its flesh."
"7782","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym informed me, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Hafsa bint Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, from A'isha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the fara'a, one out of every fifty."
"7783","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No fara' and no atira. And the fara' is the first offspring; it would be produced for them and they would slaughter it."
"7784","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the atira. He said: We used to slaughter a sheep in Rajab in ignorance, calling it the atira; shall we slaughter it today? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Slaughter for Allah in whatever month it is, do good to Allah, and feed. Ayyub said: So Ibn Sirin used to slaughter the atira in the month of Rajab. And others of the people of Mecca said: And Ibn Sirin used to narrate something about it."
"7785","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: The people of ignorance used to slaughter for every household in Rajab a sheep, calling it the atira. When Islam came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked men, among them Abdullah ibn Amr, and they said: Something we used to do in ignorance, O Messenger of Allah, we called it the atira, and we used to slaughter it for every household in Rajab; shall we do it in Islam? He said: Yes, and call it al-Rajabiyya."
"7786","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed us, from Habib ibn Mikhnaf al-Anbari, from his father, he said: I reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Arafa while he was saying: Do you know it? He said: I do not know what they answered him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon the people of every house to slaughter a sheep in every Rajab, and in every Adha a sheep."
"7787","From Ibn Uyayna, from Sadaqa ibn Yasar, he said: I said to Mujahid: I heard a man in the mosque of Kufa saying: By the Lord of this mosque, I slaughtered the atira in ignorance and Islam. So he asked me where I heard this? He said: I said: In the mosque of Kufa. He said: I have not seen a land more fitting to hear knowledge in unheard-of than the mosque of Kufa, or he said: Kufa."
"7788","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What about jiwar and i'tikaf, are they different or one thing? He said: Rather they are different. The houses of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were in the mosque. When he performed i'tikaf in the month of Ramadan, he went out from his houses to the belly of the mosque and performed i'tikaf in it. I said to him: If a man says: Upon me is i'tikaf of days, is it inside it necessarily? He said: Yes. And if he says: Upon me is jiwar of days, then at its door or inside it if he wishes."
"7789","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: Jiwar and i'tikaf are one."
"7790","From Fudayl, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The Haram is all a mosque; he may perform i'tikaf in whichever of it he wishes, and if he wishes in his dwelling, except that he prays only in congregation."
"7791","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata reporting from Ya'la ibn Umayya, he said: I stay in the mosque an hour, and I stay only to perform i'tikaf. He said: And I thought Safwan ibn Ya'la informed me of it."
"7792","Ibn Jurayj said, Ata said: It is i'tikaf as long as he stays in it, and if he sits in the mosque seeking good, he is a mu'takif, otherwise not."
"7793","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, I think, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: No i'tikaf except in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7794","From al-Thawri, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from Sa'd ibn Ubayda, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: No i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."
"7795","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, and from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: No i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."
"7796","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama, and from a man, from al-Hasan, they used to permit i'tikaf in the tribal mosques in which prayer is established."
"7797","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: He used to see no harm in i'tikaf in these mosques, the tribal mosques. Mansur said: And Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to perform i'tikaf in the mosque of his people."
"7798","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Amir, he said: Abu al-Ahwas used to perform i'tikaf in the mosque of his people."
"7799","From al-Thawri, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from Ibrahim, he said: Hudhayfa came to Abdullah and said: Are you not amazed at people in devotion between your house and the house of al-Ash'ari? Abdullah said: Perhaps they were right and you were wrong. Hudhayfa said: I do not care whether I perform i'tikaf in it or in these houses of yours; i'tikaf is only in these three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the mosque of Medina, and al-Aqsa Mosque. And those who had performed i'tikaf, Hudhayfa faulted them, in the great mosque of Kufa."
"7800","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Arqam, from Shaddad ibn al-Azma', he said: A man performed i'tikaf in the mosque in a tent of his, and people pelted him. He said: So the man sent me to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and Abdullah came, drove the people away, and approved of that."
"7801","From Ibn Uyayna, from Jami' ibn Abi Rashid, he said: I heard Abu Wa'il saying: Hudhayfa said to Abdullah: People in devotion between your house and the house of Abu Musa, will you not forbid them? Abdullah said to him: Perhaps they were right and you were wrong, and they remembered and you forgot. Hudhayfa said: No i'tikaf except in these three mosques: the mosque of Medina, the mosque of Mecca, and the mosque of Iliya."
"7802","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: No i'tikaf except in a congregational mosque."
"7803","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: No jiwar except in a congregational mosque. Then he said: No jiwar except in the mosque of Mecca and the mosque of Medina. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: I do not think he should make jiwar in the mosque of Kufa and Basra."
"7804","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a man of the people of these waters vowed jiwar, I named for him al-Zahran and Usfan, in their mosque? He said: He fulfils it if he imposed it upon himself in that mosque. I said: He vowed jiwar in the mosque of Mina? He said: Then let him make jiwar in it, for it has distinction. I said: Does he make his building, then in Mina in the house? He said: No, because of the threshold of the door. I said: Then in our mosque likewise? He said: No, that threshold is only for the house, and it is not like this mosque of ours. Then he said after: No jiwar except in the mosque of Mecca and the mosque of Medina. He said: And the people of Basra make jiwar in their mosque, such that one of them makes jiwar of his mosque in his house."
"7805","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Then the mosque of Iliya? He said: Let him make jiwar only in the mosque of Mecca and the mosque of Medina."
"7806","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, he said: A'isha performed i'tikaf between Hira and Thabir, and we used to come to her there, with a slave of hers leading her in prayer."
"7807","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that A'isha vowed jiwar in the belly of Thabir, on the side toward Mina. I said: Did she make jiwar? He said: Yes. And Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr had forbidden her to make jiwar fearing it be taken as sunna, so A'isha said: It was a need in myself."
"7808","From Hushaym, from Mughira, he said: I asked Ibrahim about a woman who performed i'tikaf in the mosque of her house, may she pass through her shade? He said: Yes, it is a path. He said: I said: She performed i'tikaf in her shade, may she pass through her house? He said: No."
"7809","From Isra'il, from a man, from al-Sha'bi, he said: No harm if a man performs i'tikaf in the mosque of his house."
"7810","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Whoever vowed to perform i'tikaf in the mosque of Iliya and performed i'tikaf in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Medina, it suffices him. And whoever vowed to perform i'tikaf in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Medina and performed i'tikaf in the Sacred Mosque, it suffices him. Ma'mar said: And whoever vowed to perform i'tikaf on top of a mountain, he should not perform i'tikaf in it, but perform i'tikaf in a congregational mosque."
"7811","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a man vowed jiwar in Bayt al-Maqdis, does the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Medina suffice for him? He said: Yes. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar refuses that."
"7812","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He vowed jiwar in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), does it suffice him to make jiwar in the mosque of Mecca? He said: Yes. And Amr ibn Dinar refuses that."
"7813","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It was claimed that the better of the mosques is dearer to him for a man to make jiwar in it, even if he vowed jiwar in another, meaning that the better of the mosques are those in which excellence is reported: the mosque of Mecca, the mosque of Medina, and the mosque of Iliya."
"7814","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if he vowed jiwar in the mosque of Mecca, does it suffice him to make jiwar in the mosque of Medina? He said: No. I said: He vowed jiwar in the mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), does it suffice him to make jiwar in the mosque of Iliya? He said: No. I said: He vowed jiwar on the tops of these mountains, the mountains of Mecca, does it suffice him to make jiwar in the mosque? He said: Yes, the mosque is better and purer. I said: And likewise in every land? He said: Yes. Then he informed me thereupon the report of A'isha when she vowed to make jiwar in the belly of Thabir."
"7815","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Hunayn, Umar asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a vow he had vowed in ignorance, i'tikaf of a day, and he commanded him with it."
"7816","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amra, from A'isha, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to perform i'tikaf in the last ten of Ramadan. She said: So I sought his permission and he permitted me, and Hafsa sought his permission and he permitted her, and Zaynab heard of that. She said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended to perform i'tikaf, would pray Fajr then go to his place of i'tikaf, and he ordered a structure to be built, and it was pitched. She said: When he prayed Fajr, behold four structures. He said: What is this? They said: A'isha, Hafsa, and Zaynab. He said: Do you say they intend righteousness with this? And he lifted his structure. She said: So he did not perform i'tikaf in the last ten of Ramadan, and performed i'tikaf for ten of Shawwal."
"7817","Ibn Uyayna informed us, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah mentioning that a slave woman died with i'tikaf due upon her. He said: So I hastened with my brothers to Ibn Abbas and asked him. He said: Perform i'tikaf for her, and fast."
"7818","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, they said: No jiwar except with fasting."
"7819","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Abu Fakhta, freedman of Ja'da ibn Hubayra, informed him, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: The mujawir fasts, meaning the mu'takif."
"7820","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Fakhta al-Awfi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The mujawir fasts, meaning the mu'takif."
"7821","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever performs i'tikaf, fasting is upon him."
"7822","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ata, from A'isha, she said: Whoever performs i'tikaf, fasting is upon him."
"7823","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: No i'tikaf except with fasting. Ma'mar said: And al-Zuhri used to make it obligatory upon him whether he intended it or not."
"7824","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The sunna of one who performs i'tikaf is to fast."
"7825","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A woman vowed to perform i'tikaf a month in the time of Ziyad, and he used to prevent i'tikaf because of the Khawarij. So he was spoken to for her, but he refused to permit her. So they asked Shurayh. He said: She fasts and feeds a poor person each day, two rites for one rite."
"7826","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: No i'tikaf except with fasting."
"7827","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: For the mu'takif is what he stipulated at his i'tikaf. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: His stipulation stands."
"7828","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Miqsam, freedman of Abdullah ibn al-Harith, he said: Ali and Ibn Mas'ud said about the mujawir: His intention stands."
"7829","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a man vowed jiwar in himself, does he intend in himself when he vows that he will not fast, and that he will sell and buy, visit markets, visit the sick, follow funerals, and that if there is rain he will shelter in the house, relieve himself in his house, and that he will make jiwar interrupted? He said: That is upon his intention, whatever it was."
"7830","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The mu'takif stipulates Friday prayer, funerals, the sick, and if a need moves him."
"7831","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If he stipulates that he will observe itikaf by day and go home at night, that is allowed for him."
"7832","From a man, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: This is not itikaf."
"7833","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: Whoever observes itikaf, let him not utter foul speech, nor revile others, and let him attend Friday prayer and the funeral, and let him advise his family while standing if he has a need, and not sit with them. And Abd al-Razzaq holds this view."
"7834","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: The mutakif visits the sick, follows the funeral, and answers a ruler if he calls him."
"7835","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The mutakif does not go out except for an unavoidable need, such as stool or urine, and he does not follow a funeral, nor visit a sick person, nor accept an invitation, nor touch a woman nor have intimate contact with her. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, likewise."
"7836","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The mutakif does not follow a funeral, nor visit a sick person."
"7837","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: The mutakif does not accept an invitation, nor visit a sick person, nor follow a funeral, and there is no itikaf except with fasting, and no itikaf except in a congregational mosque."
"7838","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Amra, she said: Aisha during her itikaf, when she went out to her house for her need, would pass by the sick and ask about him while passing, without stopping at him."
"7839","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amra, from Aisha, she said: She would pass by a sick person among her family while passing through, and would not turn aside to him."
"7840","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: The mutakif enters the house and greets, but does not sit, and he visits the sick."
"7841","From Mamar, from Qatada, he used to permit the mutakif to visit the sick without sitting, and he used to permit him to accompany the funeral."
"7842","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, he saw no harm if the mutakif goes out for a need, then a man meets him and asks him to stop with him and question him."
"7843","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if his son or his relative dies? He said: Glory be to Allah, shall we leave him to follow his funeral and break his jiwar? I said: He prays over peoples funerals? He said: If his jiwar is at the door of the mosque, yes, and if his jiwar is inside it, no."
"7844","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if his son or his relative is sick? He said: He should not visit him unless he breaks his jiwar. I said: What if the sick one among his family comes to him in his place of mujawara, may he ask him about his complaint? He said: Yes, what harm is in that. I said: May he send him a messenger to ask about him? He said: Yes. I said: What if the sick one is in a tent at the top of the valley, may he visit him? He said: No."
"7845","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Umayya told me, from one he approves, that Aisha during her itikaf would enter her house for her need, then pass by the sick and ask about him while walking, without stopping at him."
"7846","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa told me, from his father, he said: The mutakif does not visit a sick person, nor accept an invitation, nor follow a funeral."
"7847","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Must he spend the night in the mosque? He said: No, if he has a tent at the door of the mosque, it does not harm him in whichever of them he sleeps, and it is dearer to me that he sleep in the mosque."
"7848","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Safiyya bint Huyayy, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was in itikaf, and I came to him at night and spoke to him, then I stood up, and he stood with me to return me, and her dwelling was in the room of Usama ibn Zayd. Then two men of the Ansar passed by, and when they saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, they hurried. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Take your time, she is Safiyya bint Huyayy. They said: Glory be to Allah, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Satan flows in man like blood, and I feared he might cast something into your hearts, or he said: evil."
"7849","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Muwarriq ibn Said, from Ibn al-Mualla, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was in itikaf in the mosque, and his wives gathered to him, then they dispersed, and he said to Safiyya bint Huyayy: I will take you back to your house. So he went with her until he brought her into her house, while he was in itikaf."
"7850","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: Sawda, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out one night, and Umar ibn al-Khattab saw her and said: You will not hide from us, and she was tall. That was mentioned to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was eating a bone, and he did not put it down until it was revealed to him: You have been permitted to go out at night for your needs."
"7851","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The mutakif neither sells nor buys."
"7852","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The mutakif neither sells nor buys, and he does not go out to a ruler to dispute before him unless he intends that."
"7853","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no harm if the mutakif disputes before an amir in the mosque, or settles with a debtor, or advises his family about their work and livelihood, and writes a letter about his need. And Mamar said it."
"7854","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: The mutakif does not quarrel, meaning: he does not show enmity."
"7855","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The mutakif neither sells nor buys."
"7856","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: The mujawir neither sells nor buys."
"7857","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ammar ibn Abdullah ibn Yasar, from his father, he said: Ali gave Jada ibn Hubayra six hundred dirhams, helping him with the price of a servant. Then he met him and said: Did you buy the servant? He said: I am in itikaf. He said: What is against you if you go out to the market and buy her."
"7858","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Should he dispute before an amir? He said: No. I said: If he is called? He said: He says: I am a mujawir."
"7859","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May the mujawir attend gatherings in the mosques and talk with them? He said: Yes. I said: What if his jiwar is inside the mosque, may he go out if he wishes and sit at its doors? He said: He does not go out except for a need."
"7860","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He does not go about in the land except to attend a prayer, or to go for stool."
"7861","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A debtor comes to him in his mujawara, and he settles his right with him? He said: There is no harm in it. I said: His place of mujawara is brought to him, may he buy and sell in it? He said: There is no harm in that."
"7862","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, about one who has intercourse with his wife while in itikaf, he said: Nothing has reached us about that, but we hold that he should free a neck, like the expiation of one who has intercourse with his family in Ramadan."
"7863","From Mamar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, about one who has intercourse with his wife while in itikaf? He said: He frees a neck, and if he does not find, he fasts two consecutive months, and if he cannot, feeding sixty poor."
"7864","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the mutakif has intercourse with his wife, he restarts his itikaf."
"7865","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The mutakif does not approach his family by night nor by day, meaning: he does not have intercourse with his family, nor kiss, nor have intimate contact, nor touch, nor feel, let him keep away from her as much as he can. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said it also."
